window.MANUAL_INDEX=[{"t": "Introduction", "u": "01-introduction.html", "x": "Chapter 1 Introduction RepeatViewer is a free desktop application for working with repetitive DNA: transposable elements (TEs), satellite DNA and other repeats. It puts a file browser, a sequence viewer and a set of analysis tools in one window, so that the everyday loop of looking at a sequence, running a tool, checking the result and running the next tool happens in one place, without writing command lines by hand. Figure 1.1. The RepeatViewer main window. Left: the Folders and Files panels. Right: the Preview panel showing an annotated LTR retrotransposon, with the annotation side panel open."}, {"t": "Introduction: 1.1 What RepeatViewer is for", "u": "01-introduction.html#what-repeatviewer-is-for", "x": "Automated repeat pipelines produce candidate consensus sequences that are rarely ready to publish. Boundaries are wrong, terminal repeats are cut short, alignments contain fragments and unrelated copies, and classifications need checking. This last step is manual and repetitive: many small tool runs on the same files. RepeatViewer is built for that work. You point it at a folder, click a file to see it, run a tool from the toolbar, and the result appears in the same folder, highlighted, ready to be the input of the next step. Next to TE curation it covers the work around repeats in a plant genomics lab: Sequences and annotations : view, annotate, edit and export features (LTRs, TIRs, TSDs, domains, ORFs, tandem repeats), find motifs, transfer annotations between sequences. Search and alignment : BLAST (local and NCBI), HMMER, MAFFT, MUSCLE and FastGA, with an alignment viewer and alignment cleaning. Curation : MSA Extend, MSA Clean, Consensus, boundary prediction, TSD search, TE-Aid reports. Mapping : place reads or copies on a reference with minimap2 or HISAT2 and see where they differ. Trees : build trees with IQ-TREE, FastME or FastTree and explore them in an interactive tree viewer. Repeat quantification and plots : copy numbers of satellites and TE families in genomes, density, divergence and abundance plots, dotplots. Primers and probes : design and test PCR primers and FISH probes, run in-silico PCR and look at the expected gel. Automation : workflows, plugins and command wrappers."}, {"t": "Introduction: 1.2 What RepeatViewer is not", "u": "01-introduction.html#what-repeatviewer-is-not", "x": "RepeatViewer does not discover TEs in a raw genome and does not build a TE library from scratch. That is the job of tools such as TEtrimmer , of which RepeatViewer started as the interactive companion. RepeatViewer works equally well on any folder of sequences and does not need TEtrimmer."}, {"t": "Introduction: 1.3 Names", "u": "01-introduction.html#names", "x": "The application is called RepeatViewer . The Python package and the command you type in a terminal are repeatviewer ."}, {"t": "Introduction: 1.4 Authors", "u": "01-introduction.html#authors", "x": "This manual was written by Abdullah Yusuf, Nusrat Sultana, Jiangzhao Qian and Tony Heitkam (Molecular Botany, RWTH Aachen University, Aachen, Germany)."}, {"t": "Introduction: 1.5 How to read this manual", "u": "01-introduction.html#how-to-read-this-manual", "x": "The chapters follow the kind of work you do, not the order of the toolbar: Chapter Covers Installation Installing RepeatViewer and the optional external programs Quick start One complete curation pass, step by step The interface Panels, toolbar, Edit menu, Find, keyboard shortcuts, how every tool behaves Sequences Viewing sequences, the Overview, sequence operations, NCBI downloads Annotations Feature detection, editing, transfer, export, Sequence Annotation, TSD search Search Manage Database, BLAST, Online BLAST, HMMER Alignments Align, the alignment viewer, MSA Clean, MSA Extend, Consensus Mapping Mapping reads or copies to a reference and reading the result Trees Building trees and the tree viewer Repeat analysis TE-Aid, dotplots, Repeat Quantify, genome plots Primers and PCR Primer design, primers on a sequence, in-silico PCR, gels, the primer database Automation Workflows, plugins, command wrappers, command line Settings Preferences and the toolbar Reference Output files, troubleshooting, citation Conventions: Menu paths are written in bold with arrows: Tools → Align → MAFFT . Keyboard shortcuts are given for macOS first, then Windows and Linux: ⌘F / Ctrl+F . File names in output tables use <name> for the full name of the input file, extension included. A round ? button next to an option in any RepeatViewer window opens a short help text for that option. This manual explains how the parts fit together; the ? texts give the detail for each setting. The screenshots were taken from synthetic demo data (a made-up Ty3/gypsy element, satellite monomers and simulated nanopore reads), so the numbers in them have no biological meaning."}, {"t": "Installation", "u": "02-installation.html", "x": "Chapter 2 Installation RepeatViewer runs on macOS, Linux and Windows and needs Python 3.10 or newer . Installing from the source code is the recommended way for now; ready-made packages for each system are in preparation."}, {"t": "Installation: 2.1 macOS and Linux", "u": "02-installation.html#macos-and-linux", "x": "Open Terminal and run: git clone https://github.com/qjiangzhao/RepeatViewer.git cd RepeatViewer conda create -n repeatviewer \"python>=3.10\" -y conda activate repeatviewer pip install -e . Start RepeatViewer with: conda activate repeatviewer repeatviewer From the repository folder, python RepeatViewer.py works too. pip install -e . also installs the RepeatViewer build of Flexidot, which makes the interactive HTML dotplots. The Flexidot on PyPI only writes images."}, {"t": "Installation: 2.2 Windows", "u": "02-installation.html#windows", "x": "Install with the normal Windows Python (not inside WSL), in PowerShell (not Command Prompt, not Ubuntu/WSL). Put RepeatViewer in a folder you can write to, such as Documents , not under C:\\Program Files . Install Python 3.10 or later from python.org . Keep py launcher ticked and tick Add python.exe to PATH . Other Pythons (from Rtools, MSYS2, Git for Windows, Cygwin) do not work: PySide6 cannot be installed in their environments. Install the Microsoft C++ Build Tools, needed by one dependency (orfipy). Download the repository from GitHub ( Code → Download ZIP ) and unzip it, for example to Documents\\RepeatViewer-main . In PowerShell, go to that folder, create an environment called .venv , and install. The commands for steps 2 to 4: winget install -e --id Microsoft.VisualStudio.BuildTools --override \"--passive --wait --add Microsoft.VisualStudio.Workload.VCTools --includeRecommended\" cd $HOME\\Documents\\RepeatViewer-main py -0p py -3 -m venv .venv .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install -U pip .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install -e . py -0p lists the python.org Pythons the launcher can use; if it is empty, install Python first. Start RepeatViewer with: .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe RepeatViewer.py Do not name the environment repeatviewer . The project already has a folder called repeatviewer with the program code; an environment with that name is written into it, and deleting the environment deletes RepeatViewer. If .venv\\Scripts\\python.exe does not exist after step 4, the environment was made by a non-Windows Python. Remove the .venv folder, install Python from python.org, and try again. The README has the full Windows notes."}, {"t": "Installation: 2.3 What is bundled", "u": "02-installation.html#what-is-bundled", "x": "These programs ship inside RepeatViewer for macOS, Linux and Windows. You do not need to install them: Program Used by BLAST+ (blastn, blastp, blastx, makeblastdb, rpstblastn) BLAST, TE-Aid, Repeat Quantify, annotation MAFFT Align, Tree, boundary prediction MUSCLE Align minimap2 2.31 Mapping IQ-TREE 3.1.4 Tree Python libraries installed with RepeatViewer cover the rest: Primer3 (primer design), pyhmmer (HMMER searches), pytrf (tandem repeats), orfipy (ORFs), Biopython."}, {"t": "Installation: 2.4 Optional external programs", "u": "02-installation.html#optional-external-programs", "x": "Some tools call programs that are too large to bundle or are only needed by a few people. Install the ones you need into a conda environment: Program Needed for Install RepeatMasker Repeat Quantify with the RepeatMasker engine conda install -c bioconda -c conda-forge repeatmasker FastGA 1.5 or newer Align → FastGA conda install -c bioconda fastga=1.5.1 HISAT2 2.2 or newer Mapping short reads conda install -c conda-forge -c bioconda hisat2=2.2.3 FastME, FastTree Tree with these methods conda install -c bioconda fastme fasttree pysam Large BAM / CRAM genome browser and Tools → Samtools Included with pip install -e . on macOS / Linux. Windows: the Win-NGS samtools bundle (no pysam wheels) bedtools Some extraction steps conda install -c bioconda bedtools AliView Tools → Open in AliView ormbunkar.se/aliview Chorus2 Chromosome painting probes see Primers and PCR You can install them into the same repeatviewer environment, or keep them in a separate one. Either way, tell RepeatViewer where they are: File → Setting → General → External tools , enter the environment name (for example repeatviewer ) or browse to the environment folder, and click Test to see which programs are found. macOS and Linux : RepeatViewer adds that environment's bin folder to the search path of every tool it runs. This matters most when RepeatViewer is not started from a terminal, because then your shell's conda setup is not loaded. Windows : the setting points at a conda environment inside WSL2 . Linux programs (RepeatMasker, FastGA) run through wsl ; the bundled BLAST, MAFFT, MUSCLE, minimap2 and IQ-TREE run natively. Most tool windows also have a Use … from the system PATH or defined Conda environment box on their Options tab, if you prefer your own copy of a bundled program."}, {"t": "Installation: 2.5 Updating", "u": "02-installation.html#updating", "x": "From the repository folder: git pull pip install -e . Run pip install -e . again after every update, because new versions sometimes add Python packages."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass", "u": "03-quick-start.html", "x": "Chapter 3 Quick start: one curation pass This chapter walks through one complete pass on one TE candidate: find its copies in the genome, align them, clean the alignment, fix the ends, rebuild the consensus and check it. It takes about fifteen minutes and touches most of the tools you will use every day."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.1 Step 1: add your folder", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-1-add-your-folder", "x": "Choose File → Add Folder… ( ⇧⌘O / Ctrl+Shift+O ), click + in the Folders panel title bar, or drag a folder from Finder or Explorer onto the Folders panel. Pick the folder that holds your candidate FASTA files: a TEtrimmer output folder, or any folder you organised yourself. It is added as a top-level folder in the Folders panel and is remembered between sessions."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.2 Step 2: register your genome", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-2-register-your-genome", "x": "Most curation tools compare your candidate with the genome it came from. Choose File → Manage Database… ( ⇧⌘G / Ctrl+Shift+G ), click Add… , choose the genome FASTA with type FASTA DNA , and click Set as reference genome . RepeatViewer builds the BLAST index once, in the background. See Manage Database for details."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.3 Step 3: look at the candidate", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-3-look-at-the-candidate", "x": "Click a FASTA file in the Files panel. The Preview panel shows it. For a single sequence there are three views: Text : the sequence itself. Overview : a map of the sequence with its annotations (LTRs, TIRs, ORFs, domains). The side panel on the right detects, adds and edits features. Dotplot (Self) : the sequence against itself. Terminal repeats show as short diagonals in the corners; tandem repeats as parallel lines. Run Feature detection in the side panel ( Annotation tab) to find LTRs, TIRs, tandem repeats and ORFs in a few seconds. See Annotations ."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.4 Step 4: find the copies", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-4-find-the-copies", "x": "With the file selected, click BLAST on the toolbar. RepeatViewer searches the candidate against the reference genome and writes two files next to it: <name>_bn.txt : the hit table. <name>_bn.txt.fa : the hit sequences cut out of the genome. This is the input for the next step. New files are highlighted green in the Files panel, and the file they were made from light blue , so you can see at once what just appeared."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.5 Step 5: align and clean", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-5-align-and-clean", "x": "Select the _bn.txt.fa file and click Align . MAFFT (bundled) aligns the copies and writes <name>_mafft.fa , which opens in the alignment viewer. Genomic copies always include rows that do not belong: fragments, unrelated insertions, rows that are mostly gaps. Click MSA Clean to remove them. Each cleaning step writes its own file, so you can see what each step removed."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.6 Step 6: fix the ends", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-6-fix-the-ends", "x": "If the alignment runs to its edge and the copies keep agreeing, the candidate is too short. Click MSA Extend to add genomic sequence on both sides of every copy, then clean again and look at the new ends. Repeat until the alignment ends where the copies stop agreeing. The Boundary prediction section in the alignment viewer's side panel suggests where the element starts and ends (from LTRs, TIRs, alignment identity, genome coverage or a poly-A tail) and draws these positions on the alignment."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.7 Step 7: rebuild the consensus", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-7-rebuild-the-consensus", "x": "Click Consensus . The alignment is collapsed into one sequence at the majority threshold you set (default 0.8), for example CroSat2.fa_mafft.fa_0.8_co.fa . Several thresholds can sit next to each other in the folder for comparison."}, {"t": "Quick start: one curation pass: 3.8 Step 8: check the result", "u": "03-quick-start.html#step-8-check-the-result", "x": "TE-Aid makes an HTML report of the new consensus: genome copies, their coverage along the consensus, self-dotplot, terminal repeats and ORFs. Dotplot compares the old and the new consensus. Feature detection on the new consensus should now find complete terminal repeats. If the ends are clean and the terminal repeats complete, the candidate is finished. If not, go back to step 6. Tip. Once the steps are routine, build them into a workflow ( Workflows → Design and manage your workflows ) and run them on a whole folder at once. See Automation ."}, {"t": "The interface", "u": "04-interface.html", "x": "Chapter 4 The interface The main window has three panels, a toolbar, a menu bar and a status bar. By default the Folders and Files panels sit on the left and the Preview panel on the right. Figure 4.1. The main window: menu bar and toolbar at the top, Folders and Files on the left, Preview on the right, status bar at the bottom."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.1 Folders panel", "u": "04-interface.html#folders-panel", "x": "Shows the folders you added (top-level folders) and their subfolders. Control What it does + Add a folder as a new top-level folder ◀ ▶ Back and forward through folders you visited ↑ Go to the parent folder Filter by name… Show only folders whose name contains the text × Hide the panel ( View → Folders shows it again) Each folder shows two numbers, for example (3/6) : files that pass the Files filter, out of all files in the folder. Right-click a folder to add or create folders, rename it, paste files into it, give it a colour, or remove a top-level folder from the list. Removing only hides it from the panel; nothing is deleted on disk. File → Restore deleted files brings removed top-level folders back, and ⌘Z undoes the removal right away."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.2 Files panel", "u": "04-interface.html#files-panel", "x": "Lists the files of the selected folder. Click a file to open it in Preview. Select several files with ⌘ -click or Shift -click, or tick their boxes, for tools that take more than one input. Figure 4.2. The Files panel after a run: the input (light blue) and the new file (green). Filter by name… shows only matching files. Columns chooses extra columns: length, number of sequences, GC %, type, description, modified date, size, colour and cytogenetic images. By default only the name is shown; the default set is chosen in File → Setting → General → Files columns . Related files sit together: a FASTA and the outputs made from it are grouped by name, so CroSat2.fa , CroSat2.fa_bn.txt and CroSat2.fa_bn.txt.fa appear one after another. Drag a GFF, GFF3, GTF or BED file onto a FASTA row to attach it as an annotation (see Annotations ). Files can be dragged to other folders in the Folders panel, or to Finder and other applications. Right-click a file for the actions that fit it: Figure 4.3. The right-click menu of a FASTA file. Item What it does Open in Preview / Open in Additional Tab Show the file in the current Preview tab, or in a new tab next to it Open in Text Editor Edit the file as plain text in Preview Find in this document… Open Find for this file (see Find ) Rename Rename the file ( F2 ) Add as BLAST Database… Index this FASTA for BLAST (see Manage Database ) Add Annotation… / Export Annotation… Attach a GFF/BED file, or write the annotations to GFF3, GTF, BED or a figure Attach Cytogenetic Image… Link a microscope image (FISH, karyotype) to this sequence Extract Sequences from File Pick records from a multi-FASTA (see Sequences ) Separate Sequence into N Files / Folders Split a multi-FASTA, one record per file or folder Combine N Sequences to One File Merge the selected FASTA files New Folder with Selected Items Move the selection into a new folder Cut, Copy, Copy Path, Paste, Duplicate, Delete File operations; all can be undone Open in AliView Open an alignment in AliView Show in File Manager / Reveal in Finder Show the file in Finder or Explorer File Color Mark the file with a colour"}, {"t": "The interface: 4.3 Preview panel", "u": "04-interface.html#preview-panel", "x": "Shows the selected file. What you see depends on the file type: File View FASTA, one sequence Text , Overview (annotated map) and Dotplot (Self) sub-tabs FASTA, several sequences Overview of all records, one row each Aligned FASTA Alignment viewer (see Alignments ) Tree ( .treefile , .nwk , .newick ) Tree viewer (see Trees ) SAM, BAM, CRAM, GFF, GTF, BED Genome browser (see Mapping ) PAF, .1aln Alignment dotplot .rvgel In-silico gel (see Primers and PCR ) HTML reports TE-Aid, tree and mapping reports, plots, shown live PDF, images, text, tables Shown as they are The Actions button in the Preview title bar has: Reset scale : back to the default zoom. Edit text / Save text : edit a text or FASTA file in place. If you leave a file with unsaved typing, RepeatViewer asks whether to save it. Duplicate window : a copy of the current Preview in its own window. Split window : two Preview panes side by side, to compare two files. Right-click a Preview tab to open it in the right or left half of a split. Open in Additional Tab (right-click a file) keeps the current tab and adds another one, which is the quickest way to compare a before and an after. HTML reports have a zoom box and Open in browser in the title bar."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.4 Toolbar", "u": "04-interface.html#toolbar", "x": "The tools you use most, as buttons. The default set is BLAST, Align, Dotplot, TE-Aid, Mapping, Tree, Primers and Probes and Visualization. Choose which buttons appear, and in which order, in File → Setting → Toolbar . Most buttons do two things: Click : run the tool on the selected files with your saved settings, or open its window when it needs choices every time. Right-click : the other of the two. What right-click does is set on the Options tab of each tool's window (\"Right-click action\"). Buttons with a small arrow open a menu (for example Visualization)."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.5 Status bar", "u": "04-interface.html#status-bar", "x": "The bottom line of the window shows the name of the last previewed file (click it to show the file in Finder or Explorer), or the result of the last job. While a tool runs it shows Running … , a blue progress bar and a Stop button. For files with many sequences the bar advances sequence by sequence and says how many are done and how many are left."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.6 The Edit menu", "u": "04-interface.html#the-edit-menu", "x": "The Edit menu acts on the panel you are working in: the Files panel, the Folders panel, the tree viewer, or the text field that has the cursor. Figure 4.4. The Edit menu. Undo and Redo ( ⌘Z , ⇧⌘Z ) cover file actions: delete, paste, move, overwrite, rename, Batch Rename, duplicate, and removing a folder from the list. A copy that you changed after it was made is never deleted by Undo. In a text being edited, Undo first undoes your typing. Cut, Copy, Paste work on files in the Files and Folders panels, and on text in text fields. Paste Reverse Complement ( ⇧⌘V ) pastes the reverse complement of the DNA on the clipboard into a text field or a sequence being edited. IUPAC codes, case, gaps and line width are kept; FASTA headers get \" reverse_complement\" added. Protein on the clipboard is not pasted. Rename… ( F2 ) renames the selected file or folder. Batch Rename… renames many files at once: find and replace (with Match Case and Regular Expression , where \\1 refers to a group), add a prefix or suffix, and keep the file extension ( .fa.gz stays together). The table shows every new name before anything changes; names that are taken, repeated or invalid are shown in red and Rename stays off until all are fine. Annotation files, mapping records, file colours and image links follow their files. One Undo puts everything back. Figure 4.5. Batch Rename: the new names are shown before anything is renamed. Duplicate copies the selected files or folders. File Color marks files with a colour. Clear Highlights removes the blue and green job highlights."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.7 Find and Go To", "u": "04-interface.html#find-and-go-to", "x": "Edit → Find in this document… ( ⌘F ) searches the file in Preview. Figure 4.6. Find with hits listed. The current hit is selected in the sequence view. Sequence : a motif in Nucleotides , or in Protein (6 frames) (the DNA is translated in all six frames). Include reverse complement searches both strands. Interpret IUPAC codes : R matches A or G, N matches anything, in the motif and in the sequence. Allow gaps in sequence : gaps in alignment rows are skipped, so a motif is found across them. Max mismatches : how many differences a hit may have. Sequence names and Annotation names : also match record names and feature names. Find All lists every hit (up to 100,000). Find Next ( F3 or ⌘G ) and Find Previous ( ⇧F3 ) move through them; the view jumps to each hit and keeps your zoom when the letters are readable. Save Hits as Annotations… adds the hits as features. Large files are searched in the background with a progress bar and Stop . Closing Find removes the highlights. The same search is in the Find tab of the Overview side panel, for quick searches without a separate window. Edit → Go To Position… ( ⌥⌘G ) jumps to a position or range: 1200 , 1000-2000 or scaffold_5:1000-2000 ."}, {"t": "The interface: 4.8 Keyboard shortcuts", "u": "04-interface.html#keyboard-shortcuts", "x": "macOS Windows / Linux Action ⇧⌘O Ctrl+Shift+O Add Folder ⇧⌘N Ctrl+Shift+N New Folder ⇧⌘G Ctrl+Shift+G Manage Database ⌘, Ctrl+, Settings ⌘Z Ctrl+Z Undo ⇧⌘Z Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo ⌘X, ⌘C, ⌘V Ctrl+X, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V Cut, Copy, Paste ⇧⌘V Ctrl+Shift+V Paste Reverse Complement ⌘A Ctrl+A Select All F2 F2 Rename ⌘F Ctrl+F Find (in the Files or Folders panel: focus its filter box) F3 or ⌘G F3 or Ctrl+G Find Next ⇧F3 Shift+F3 Find Previous ⌥⌘G Ctrl+Alt+G Go To Position F5 or ⌘R F5 Refresh the current folder Delete or Backspace Delete Delete the selected files (asks first; can be undone) ⌘Q Ctrl+Q Quit In the sequence, alignment and tree views: Input Overview, alignment, tree, dotplot Genome browser (mapping) Scroll Move through the view Move through the view Shift + scroll Zoom around the mouse Scroll up and down through the rows ⌘ + scroll, or pinch Zoom around the mouse − / + buttons, slider Zoom Zoom Double-click empty space Fit the whole sequence or tree"}, {"t": "The interface: 4.9 How every tool behaves", "u": "04-interface.html#how-every-tool-behaves", "x": "The same rules hold for every tool. Knowing them saves reading each tool's section closely. Outputs land next to their input. A tool run on /data/run2/CroSat2.fa writes into /data/run2/ . The next tool in the curation loop takes the last output as its input, so they belong together. Output names keep the full input name. A suffix is added to the whole name, extension included: CroSat2.fa → CroSat2.fa_mafft.fa . You can always tell which file a result came from. Tools that make many files put only the main result next to the input and the rest into a <name>_details folder. Nothing is overwritten. A second run on the same input writes _2 , _3 , and so on. When you name a file yourself and the name is taken, RepeatViewer asks whether to Overwrite , Cancel or Rename… . Highlights show what happened. During a run the input files are blue and new files green. Afterwards the inputs are light blue, the new files green, and the main result opens in Preview, selected in deep blue. Jobs run in the background. You can keep browsing and previewing while a tool runs. Several jobs can run at the same time, up to Max. parallel jobs in File → Setting → General → Performance . Stop in the status bar cancels. Selection drives the tools. Tools act on the files selected in the Files panel. In each tool window, Inputs offers Use selected sequences or Define inputs (other files for this run only). Click a file to fill a field. While a tool window is open, clicking a file in the Files panel fills the path field you are working on, for example a reference or a genome. Browse works too. Windows look the same. Each tool window has the tabs of its programs plus an Options tab (what right-click runs, whether to show the process window, whether to use your own copy of the program). Buttons are always Restore Defaults and Advanced Settings ▸ on the left, Cancel , Save and Run on the right. Save keeps the settings without running. Settings are remembered between sessions. Every tool is in the Tools menu. Click a tool name (BLAST, Mapping, …) to open its window — same as the toolbar. Choose the program or operation on the window tabs, then Run . Right-click the toolbar button to run with saved settings. Figure 4.7. The Tools menu, grouped by kind of work."}, {"t": "Sequences", "u": "05-sequences.html", "x": "Chapter 5 Sequences"}, {"t": "Sequences: 5.1 Viewing a sequence", "u": "05-sequences.html#viewing-a-sequence", "x": "Click a FASTA file with one sequence. Preview shows three sub-tabs. Text The sequence as text, with its length and GC content in the status line. Use Actions → Edit text to change it in place and Actions → Save text to save. The font size is set in File → Setting → General → Files & preview . Figure 5.1. The Text view. Overview A map of the sequence: a ruler, the bases (coloured, readable when you zoom in), and the annotations as arrows in tracks. LTRs, TIRs, TSDs, ORFs, domains and tandem repeats each have their own colour. Figure 5.2. The Overview of an LTR retrotransposon: LTRs (red), TSDs (blue ends), domains (blue arrows) and the gag-pol ORF (green). The bar above the map: − / + and the slider zoom; Fit shows the whole sequence. Position … to … shows the selected range; type numbers to select a range, Select selects it. Length and GC of the sequence. Click a feature to select it; drag on the ruler to select a range; double-click a position to zoom in to the bases. Hover a feature for its details. The side panel on the right has five tabs (click a tab again to hide the panel): Tab Holds Display What is drawn and how: tracks, colours, sizes Annotation Feature detection, similarity annotation, manual features, transfer, Delete, Restore, Export, Save (see Annotations ) Find Motif search in this sequence (see Find ) Others Reserved for extra options Primers Primers on this sequence and PCR (see Primers and PCR ) Figure 5.3. The Display tab of the side panel. Display has three sections: Tracks : Show complement (the second strand under the first), Show translation (amino acids in the reading frames you choose), Split annotation by source (one track per program or file that made the features), and Displayed annotations : All , then one tick per feature type in its colour (LTR, ORF, TSD, PBS, domain …). Setting chooses the colour of each type. Color : the colour scheme for bases ( Nucleotide ) and amino acids ( Protein , for example Clustal). Size : font sizes of base and amino-acid letters, annotation labels and the ruler, and the thickness and spacing of annotation arrows. Dotplot (Self) The sequence against itself. The main diagonal is the sequence matching itself. Short diagonals parallel to it in the corners are direct terminal repeats (LTRs); short lines at right angles are inverted repeats (TIRs); blocks of parallel lines are tandem repeats. Figure 5.4. The self dotplot of the demo element. In the Display panel: Word size (length of exact matches drawn; smaller finds more and is noisier), Line width , Show reverse (inverted matches) and X / Y ranges with View to zoom to a region. Export saves the plot as a figure. Zooming and moving never recalculate the plot; only a new word size does."}, {"t": "Sequences: 5.2 Several sequences in one file", "u": "05-sequences.html#several-sequences-in-one-file", "x": "A FASTA with several records opens as an overview: one row per record, with the record names on the left and their annotations on the right, all on one ruler. Click a row to select a record; the side panel then works on that record. Figure 5.5. A multi-FASTA overview of 24 satellite monomers. To work on one record by itself, right-click the file and choose Separate Sequence into N Files (or Folders ), or extract the records you want (see below). An aligned FASTA (all rows the same length, with gaps) opens in the alignment viewer instead; see Alignments ."}, {"t": "Sequences: 5.3 Large genomes and compressed files", "u": "05-sequences.html#large-genomes-and-compressed-files", "x": "A FASTA larger than 1 GB (256 MB when gzipped) does not load at once. Preview shows its sequences instead: the file is indexed once (a .fai next to it, the same index samtools writes; a progress bar shows how far it is) and every sequence is listed with its length and its number of saved annotations. Only the part you open is read from disk, so a 4 GB genome opens in seconds after the first index. Double-click a sequence: up to 100 Mb opens whole, a longer one opens its first 5 Mb. Region : type scaffold_5:120,000,000-125,000,000 , a name alone (the whole sequence) or 1000-2000 (the selected sequence), then Open Region . At most 100 Mb. Open Region and Open Whole Sequence write the part next to the genome as <genome>_<name>_<start>-<end>.fa (whole sequence: <genome>_<name>.fa ) and open it. The header keeps the genome positions ( >scaffold_5:120000001-125000000 from Csat_v1.fa.gz ). Opening the same region again reuses the file. Extract to File writes the region to a name you choose. Compressed files: every FASTA name ending in .gz or .bgz ( .fa.gz , .fasta.gz , .fna.gz ...) opens like the plain file, and compressed text files ( .gff3.gz , .tsv.gz ...) open as text. A bgzip file (made by bgzip or samtools ) opens regions as fast as a plain file (index .fai + .gzi next to it). A plain gzip file has to be read from its start for every region; Preview then offers Convert to bgzip , which writes <name>_bgzip.fa.gz once. Tip. .fna , .ffn and .fsa files are FASTA too."}, {"t": "Sequences: 5.4 Sequence operations", "u": "05-sequences.html#sequence-operations", "x": "The Sequence menu holds operations that make new FASTA files. New Sequence Type or paste a sequence, give it a name (required), a description and an organism, choose Nucleotide (DNA) or Protein , and click OK . The file is written into the current folder ( .fa for DNA, .faa for protein). Figure 5.6. New Sequence. Combine Sequences to One File Select several FASTA files and merge them into one multi-FASTA, for example to put curated consensus sequences together into a library. Extract Sequences from File Picks records out of a multi-FASTA. Figure 5.7. Extract Sequences: tick the records to extract. Pick from list : tick records (filter by name, Select all , Select none ). From ID list : give a text file with one name per line. Large files are read in the background. Write all selected into one file : one output ( <name>_extr.fa ) or one file per record. Copy annotations for extracted sequences : the features of each record go with it. Extract subject sequences from hit table Select a BLAST hit table ( _bn.txt ) and choose this item (also on the table's right-click menu) to cut the hit regions out of the genome the search ran against. The genome is found from the link RepeatViewer writes next to BLAST results, then from the project genome; only if both are missing are you asked for it. Reverse Complement Writes <name>_rc.fa . Candidates are often built on the wrong strand; check this before you curate the ends. ORF Prediction Finds open reading frames and writes <name>_orfs.fa . Setting Default Meaning Min ORF length 30 bp Shortest ORF reported Max ORF length 10000 bp Longest ORF reported Add other start codons none Extra start codons besides ATG, for example TTG, CTG Reading frames all six Which frames to search Figure 5.8. ORF Prediction settings. ORFs are also found by Feature detection in the Overview, which is usually the better place to look at them. Translate Writes the protein translation as <name>_translated.faa ."}, {"t": "Sequences: 5.5 Download from NCBI", "u": "05-sequences.html#download-from-ncbi", "x": "Tools → Download from NCBI searches NCBI and downloads sequences, proteins or whole genome assemblies into the current folder, like Geneious' NCBI search. Figure 5.9. Download from NCBI. Choose the Database : Nucleotide, Protein or Genome Assembly. Type accessions (separated by spaces, commas or new lines; they are found and ticked directly) or a search term. Entrez syntax works, for example Crocus[Organism] AND satellite . GCA_/GCF_ accessions switch to Genome Assembly by themselves. Narrow the search with Organism , Length , Molecule and RefSeq only ; click Search . Results load page by page as you scroll. Tick the records you want, choose the Format and whether to save One file or One file per record , and click Run . Database Formats Nucleotide FASTA ( .fa ), FASTA with NCBI annotations, GenBank ( .gb ) Protein FASTA ( .faa ), GenPept ( .gp ) Genome Assembly FASTA, optionally with the GFF3 annotation ( <name>.gff3 ) FASTA with NCBI annotations turns NCBI's feature table into RepeatViewer annotations, so the genes and features show in the Overview at once. File names: one record → <accession>.fa ; several in one file → named after the search; genomes → <GCA accession>_<assembly name>.fa . If files with these names exist, you are asked whether to overwrite or skip them. Downloads run on their own, outside the normal job queue: each has its own progress bar and Stop button in the status bar, and other tools keep working. Further downloads wait in a queue. On the Options tab, an NCBI API key raises the request limit from 3 to 10 per second, and the right-click action can download accessions copied to the clipboard with the saved settings."}, {"t": "Annotations", "u": "06-annotations.html", "x": "Chapter 6 Annotations Annotations (features) are named regions of a sequence: LTRs, TIRs, TSDs, protein domains, ORFs, tandem repeats, primer sites, or anything you mark by hand. They are shown in the Overview and in the alignment viewer, and can be moved between sequences and exported."}, {"t": "Annotations: 6.1 Where annotations are stored", "u": "06-annotations.html#where-annotations-are-stored", "x": "Every annotation of name.fa is saved in name.seqmap_RV.json next to it, whether it came from feature detection, a similarity search, Find, a TSD search or your own hand. This file keeps the colours, types and sources of the features and is the one true copy. It is hidden in the Files panel and follows its FASTA when you rename, move, copy or delete it. GFF3, GTF and BED files are written only when you export (see Export ). To get a GFF3 on every save as well, tick Also write GFF3 on every Save in the export window; the file is then name.fa_RV.gff3 . Adding an existing annotation file Drag a GFF, GFF3, GTF or BED file onto the FASTA in the Files panel, or right-click the FASTA and choose Add Annotation… . Features are matched to records by sequence name (GFF column 1 = the FASTA header up to the first space), so the annotation file can have any name. The file itself is not moved or changed."}, {"t": "Annotations: 6.2 The Annotation tab", "u": "06-annotations.html#the-annotation-tab", "x": "Open a FASTA, switch to Overview , and open the Annotation tab of the side panel. It has four sections; click a title to open it. Figure 6.1. The Annotation tab with Feature detection open. Feature detection Finds structural features in the current sequence in a few seconds, without any database. Tick what to look for and click Run : Type What is found LTR Long terminal repeats: direct repeats at both ends TIR Terminal inverted repeats Tandem Tandem repeats (with pytrf) ORF Open reading frames TSD Target site duplications next to LTRs or TIRs PBS Primer binding sites (tRNA matches) just inside a 5′ LTR Setting opens the parameters of each detector (minimum lengths, identities, ORF size and so on). Similarity annotation Searches the sequence against your annotation databases (protein domains such as CDD or REXdb, HMM profiles such as Pfam or Dfam, or a TE library) and adds the hits as features. Which databases appear here is set in File → Manage Database with the Annotation tick (see Manage Database ). Setting sets E-values and other thresholds. Figure 6.2. Similarity annotation lists the databases marked for annotation. Manually add feature Select a range on the ruler (drag), tick the feature type, and click Add . Setting chooses which types are listed. Figure 6.3. Manually add feature. Right-click a feature for Modify (name, type, strand, range), Change color , Copy sequence , Extract Sequence (write it to a new FASTA), Attach cytogenetic image… and Delete . Drag a selected feature's end to change its range. Right-click a selected range of the ruler for Add feature… , Copy Sequence and Extract Sequence . Transfer annotation Copies annotations from an annotated sequence (the source) onto the current sequence, for example from a curated reference element onto a new copy. Figure 6.4. Transfer annotation. Source : click an annotated FASTA in the Files panel (or a folder in the Folders panel to use every annotated sequence in it), or Browse . Identity and Coverage : how similar a feature's sequence must be, and how much of it must be found, to be transferred. Allow reverse strand : also transfer onto the other strand. Selected features only (when shown): transfer only the features selected on the source. The whole source is lifted onto the target first (MAFFT for short sequences, minimap2 for long ones), and any features left over are placed by BLAST. Transferred features remember their identity and coverage. Delete, Restore, Export, Save Delete : remove the selected features. Restore : go back to the last saved state. Save : write the annotations to name.seqmap_RV.json . The button reads Save • while there are unsaved changes, and RepeatViewer asks before you leave a file with unsaved annotation changes. Export : see below."}, {"t": "Annotations: 6.3 Export", "u": "06-annotations.html#export", "x": "Export in the Annotation tab, in the alignment viewer and in the multi-sequence overview, and Export Annotation… on a FASTA's right-click menu, all open the same window. Figure 6.5. Export: choose the type and the file. Type Writes GFF3, GTF, BED The annotations as on screen, including unsaved changes PDF, SVG The current view as a vector figure, ready for a journal PNG The current view as an image, at the DPI you choose The suggested file name follows the type: name.fa_RV.gff3 for annotations, name.fa_annotation.pdf for figures."}, {"t": "Annotations: 6.4 Sequence Annotation (many files at once)", "u": "06-annotations.html#sequence-annotation-many-files-at-once", "x": "Tools → Annotate runs feature detection and similarity annotation on all selected files in one go, writing the results into each file's annotations. Use it to annotate a whole library before you look at the elements one by one. Figure 6.6. The Sequence Annotation window. Feature detection : the same types as in the side panel (including TSD search settings under Advanced). Similarity annotation : the databases marked for annotation. Threads and, under Advanced Settings , the detector parameters."}, {"t": "Search", "u": "07-search.html", "x": "Chapter 7 Search"}, {"t": "Search: 7.1 Manage Database", "u": "07-search.html#manage-database", "x": "File → Manage Database… ( ⇧⌘G / Ctrl+Shift+G ) keeps the genomes, sequence libraries and profile databases your searches use. The same window opens from the Manage Database buttons in the BLAST, HMMER, TE-Aid and MSA Extend windows. Figure 7.1. Manage Database. The window has three tabs: Tab Holds Used by BLAST database FASTA DNA (genomes, TE libraries) and FASTA Protein (protein libraries such as REXdb) BLAST, TE-Aid, MSA Extend, similarity annotation HMM database HMM DNA and HMM Protein profile files (Pfam, Dfam) HMMER, similarity annotation CDD NCBI Conserved Domain Database folders Similarity annotation (RPS-BLAST) Columns: Name (defaults to the file name), Short name (up to 15 characters, the label used for its features; - when empty), Type , Path / source , Time (when it was added) and Annotation (tick to use it in similarity annotation). Click a header to sort; type in the filter box to find an entry. Add… : choose a file and its Type . The type decides which tab it goes to; every type can be added from every tab. While the window is open, clicking a FASTA in the Files panel fills the path. BLAST indexes are built in the background into the BLAST databases folder set in File → Setting → General . Remove : forget the selected entries (the original files stay). Set as reference genome : make the selected FASTA DNA the project genome. Tools that compare with \"the genome\" (BLAST, MSA Extend, TE-Aid, boundary prediction, genome plots) use it. The current reference genome is named under the table. A chromosome sizes file is written for every FASTA DNA database. Annotation : tick to show the database in the Annotation tab's similarity search. A FASTA can also be added straight from the Files panel: right-click → Add as BLAST Database… ."}, {"t": "Search: 7.2 BLAST", "u": "07-search.html#blast", "x": "Toolbar: BLAST · Tools → Search → BLAST Searches the selected sequences against the reference genome or a saved database with BLAST+ (bundled). Figure 7.2. The BLAST window, BLASTn tab. The window has a tab per program: BLASTn (DNA against DNA), BLASTp (protein against protein), BLASTx (translated DNA against protein) and Online BLAST , plus Options . Run runs the tab you are on. Each selected file is searched separately. Setting Default Meaning Database Reference genome The genome, or a saved BLAST database; Manage Database adds one -evalue 0.0001 E-value cut-off; lower keeps fewer, more significant hits Threads CPU count minus the reserve -num_threads -word_size 11 (BLASTn) Seed length. Smaller is more sensitive and much slower on repetitive genomes Max sequence number 100 At most this many hit sequences go into the extracted FASTA Top longest sequences 100 Of those, this many are the longest hits; the rest are picked at random Export BLAST hits table on Keep the tabular hit table Advanced: -gapopen, -gapextend, -penalty, -perc_identity, -qcov_hsp_perc BLAST+ defaults Gap and mismatch costs, minimum identity and query coverage Max sequence number and Top longest sequences matter for high-copy elements: without a cap, a family with thousands of copies gives an alignment too big to work with. Taking the longest copies first keeps full-length copies in the set. Option meanings follow the NCBI BLAST+ user manual . Outputs (next to the query; bn for BLASTn, bp for BLASTp, bx for BLASTx): File Contents <name>_bn.txt Tabular hit table (outfmt 6). Input for the genome plots <name>_bn.txt.fa The hit regions cut out of the genome: the input for Align <name>_bn.gff Hit positions as GFF, when GFF output is on Online BLAST The Online BLAST tab sends one sequence to NCBI's servers, for example to see whether a new satellite or TE matches anything known. Setting Meaning Program blastn, blastp, blastx, tblastn or tblastx Database NCBI databases as on the BLAST website: core_nt (default), nt, refseq, nr, swissprot and others Max hits How many hits NCBI returns Export BLAST hits table Also keep the table <name>_obn.txt Each query file must hold one sequence (split a multi-FASTA first with right-click → Separate Sequence into N Files ). The result is an HTML report, <name>_obn.html , which opens in Preview. Hits can also be added to the sequence as annotations, coloured by identity."}, {"t": "Search: 7.3 HMMER", "u": "07-search.html#hmmer", "x": "Tools → Search → HMMER Searches the selected sequences against an HMM profile database (Pfam, Dfam, REXdb domain profiles) with pyhmmer. Figure 7.3. The HMMER window. Setting Default Meaning Database An HMM database from Manage Database E-value 0.0001 Reporting threshold Threads Parallel threads Max hits 100 Most hits kept per query Export hits table on Write the hit table Protein profiles are searched against the ORFs of DNA queries. The hit table is written as <name>_hmmer.txt next to the query ( _hmmer1.txt , _hmmer2.txt , … on later runs)."}, {"t": "Alignments", "u": "08-alignments.html", "x": "Chapter 8 Alignments"}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.1 Align", "u": "08-alignments.html#align", "x": "Toolbar: Align · Tools → Align Builds a multiple sequence alignment (MSA) with MAFFT or MUSCLE (both bundled), or a whole-genome style alignment with FastGA . Tools → Align opens the window; pick the program tab and Run . Figure 8.1. The Align window, MAFFT tab. The window has the tabs MAFFT , MUSCLE , FastGA and Options ; Run runs the tab you are on. Options sets what right-clicking the Align button runs. MAFFT and MUSCLE Mode : Combine selected sequences and align : all selected files go into one alignment. Align each selected file separately : one alignment per file. Add sequences to an existing MSA (MAFFT): new sequences are added to an alignment without realigning it ( --add ). MAFFT setting Meaning Strategy FFT-NS-1, FFT-NS-2 (fast, progressive), L-INS-i, G-INS-i, E-INS-i (iterative, accurate, for up to a few hundred sequences), Auto, or Custom Sequence type Auto-detect, nucleotide or protein Threads --thread Advanced: --retree , --maxiterate Guide-tree rebuilds and refinement cycles (Custom) Advanced: --op , --ep Gap opening penalty and offset Advanced: --reorder , --adjustdirectionaccurately Output order, and turning copies from the other strand Strategy meanings follow the MAFFT algorithms page . For TE copies from both strands, switch on --adjustdirectionaccurately ; otherwise reversed copies look extremely divergent. MUSCLE setting Meaning Algorithm -align (most accurate) or -super5 (large sets) Threads -threads Advanced: guide tree permutation, perturbation seed -perm and -perturb Outputs: one file aligned → <name>_mafft.fa / <name>_muscle.fa ; combined → <name>_cmafft.fa / <name>_cmuscle.fa . The alignment opens in the alignment viewer. FastGA FastGA aligns large sequences (chromosomes, genomes) much faster than BLAST and writes alignments for a dotplot. It is not bundled; install FastGA 1.5 or newer (see Installation ). On Windows it runs in WSL. Mode Runs Output (next to the input) Each file against itself one run per selected file <file>_FastGA.paf Selected files against each other every pair (A–B, A–C, B–C) <first>_multi_FastGA.paf Selected files against a reference one run per selected file <file>_FastGA_vs_<reference>.paf , or with Write all into one file <first>_multi_FastGA_vs_<reference>.paf For against a reference : select the files to compare, open the window, then click the reference in the Files panel (or Browse ). The files compared are the ones selected when the window opened; the reference is skipped if it is among them. A grey line says what Run will do, for example \"3 FASTA selected → 3 runs against genome.fa\". The reference is indexed once and reused; with Keep genome index the index is kept in a hidden .repeatviewer_fastga folder next to the reference (about 14 GB per gigabase). Setting Meaning Output format PAF variants, PSL, or binary .1aln . PAF and .1aln open as a dotplot -T threads, -i min identity, -l min length Speed and filters -f seed cutoff Seeds more frequent than this are dropped. RepeatViewer uses 200 (FastGA: 10) so repeats stay visible -c / -s Chain coverage and chain gap Symmetric seeding ( -S ) Result does not depend on the order of the files Use soft mask Seed only in upper-case bases Temp dir ( -P ) Where FastGA writes its large temporary files"}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.2 The alignment viewer", "u": "08-alignments.html#the-alignment-viewer", "x": "An aligned FASTA opens in the alignment viewer: sequence names on the left (numbered), the alignment on the right with a ruler, coloured bases, and annotations under the rows. Figure 8.2. The alignment viewer with the Boundary prediction section open. The bar above the alignment: Control What it does − / + and slider Zoom Position … to … Shows the selected columns; type a range and click Select to zoom to it Fit Fit the longest row to the window Clean Open MSA Clean for this alignment Cons Open Consensus for this alignment Cov Build a consensus, BLAST it against the project genome and draw TE-Aid style coverage above the alignment Feat Show or hide all feature tracks Selecting: Click a name to select a row; ⌘ -click or Shift -click for more rows. Drag on the alignment to select a block of columns. Delete removes the selected rows, or the selected columns (from the selected rows or from all), after asking. ⌘Z undoes it. Right-click a sequence name for Rename sequence , Set as reference (the row the others are compared with), Copy Sequence / Copy Sequence without gap , Extract Sequence / Extract Sequence without gap , Show self-dotplot and Add feature… . The side panel has the same tabs as for a single sequence (Display, Annotation, Find, Others, Primers). In the alignment viewer the Annotation tab adds Boundary prediction . Boundary prediction Suggests where the element starts and ends in the alignment, with the methods of TEtrimmer. The alignment is first cleaned (rare bases below 1 % become gaps; gappy or poorly conserved columns are removed), then: Row How the boundary is found LTR / TIR The 0.7 consensus is BLASTed against itself. Direct repeats of at least 100 bp at both ends give an LTR, inverted repeats of at least 20 bp a TIR MSA identity Ends with low conservation are cut, then the boundary is the first and last 4-column window whose 0.8 consensus has no N Genome Cov The 0.7 consensus is BLASTed against the project genome; the boundary is where coverage rises above the background at each end. Needs a reference genome PolyA Only for files whose name contains LINE or SINE: a run of 10 or more A near the 3′ end, followed by unconserved sequence Click Run , then tick a row to draw its boundary as lines on the alignment. TSD Search with Auto detect uses these boundaries."}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.3 MSA Clean", "u": "08-alignments.html#msa-clean", "x": "Toolbar: MSA Clean · Tools → Curate → MSA Clean Removes the rows and columns that make an alignment of genomic copies hard to read. Three steps, each on its own tab, can be run alone or together ( Tools → Curate → MSA Clean → Combined MSA clean ). Figure 8.3. MSA Clean, Remove gappy columns tab. Step Removes Main settings Remove gappy columns Columns that are mostly gaps Gap threshold (0.80), similarity check gap threshold (0.40), similarity threshold (0.70), min nucleotides per column (5) Clean with divergence Rows too different from the alignment's consensus Divergence threshold, window size Clean with gaps Rows that are mostly gaps Gap proportion threshold Each step writes its own file, so you can see what each one removed and go back one step without redoing the others: File Step <name>_gap.fa Gappy columns removed <name>_rowdiv.fa Divergent rows removed <name>_rowgap.fa Gappy rows removed On the Options tab, Right-click action chooses which step (or the combined clean) runs when you right-click the toolbar button."}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.4 MSA Extend", "u": "08-alignments.html#msa-extend", "x": "Toolbar: MSA Extend · Tools → Curate → MSA Extend Adds genomic sequence to both ends of every copy in an alignment, so you can see whether the element continues beyond the current ends. This is how truncated ends and incomplete terminal repeats are recovered. Figure 8.4. MSA Extend. Setting Default Meaning Reference genome the project genome Where the flanking sequence comes from (a genome or pan-genome) Extend left of MSA 1000 bp Bases added on the 5′ side Extend right of MSA 1000 bp Bases added on the 3′ side The usual rhythm: extend, clean, look at the new ends, extend again, until the alignment ends where the copies stop agreeing."}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.5 Consensus", "u": "08-alignments.html#consensus", "x": "Toolbar: Consensus · Tools → Curate → Consensus Collapses an alignment into one sequence. Figure 8.5. Consensus. Setting Default Meaning Sequence type Auto-detect Nucleotide or protein Majority threshold 0.80 Fraction of rows that must agree for a base to be called Ambiguous base N Written where no base reaches the threshold (X for protein) Consensus needs an alignment (all rows the same length) or a single sequence; an unaligned multi-FASTA is refused with a message to align it first. Output: the threshold is part of the name, for example CroSat2.fa_mafft.fa_0.8_co.fa , so consensus sequences at several thresholds can be compared in one folder."}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.6 MSA plot", "u": "08-alignments.html#msa-plot", "x": "Tools → Visualize → MSA plot draws an overview image of an alignment for a figure: one coloured bar per row. Figure 8.6. MSA plot. Setting Meaning Sequence type Auto-detect, nucleotide or protein Output format, Quality PNG (with DPI) or PDF Sequence height, Sequence distance Row height and space between rows, in cm Advanced Colours for each base or amino acid, with presets Output: <name>_msa_plot.png or <name>_msa_plot.pdf ."}, {"t": "Alignments: 8.7 Open in AliView", "u": "08-alignments.html#open-in-aliview", "x": "Tools → Open in AliView (or right-click a file) opens the alignment in AliView , for editing by hand. Set AliView's location in File → Setting → General → Sequence tools ."}, {"t": "Mapping", "u": "09-mapping.html", "x": "Chapter 9 Mapping Tools → Mapping (add the Mapping button in File → Setting → Toolbar ) Places reads, contigs or repeat copies on a reference and shows where they differ, like Geneious' Map to Reference . Typical uses: nanopore reads on a satellite array or a TE consensus, assembled contigs on a reference element, RNA-seq reads on a TE to see which parts are expressed."}, {"t": "Mapping: 9.1 Running a mapping", "u": "09-mapping.html#running-a-mapping", "x": "Figure 9.1. The Mapping window, Minimap2 tab. Select the reads or sequences to map in the Files panel (FASTA or FASTQ, gzipped files too), or use Define inputs . Open the window and click the Reference FASTA in the Files panel (or Browse ). If a reference genome is set, it is filled in. Choose the mapper tab, check the settings, click Run . Two mappers: minimap2 (bundled, version 2.31): long reads (ONT, PacBio HiFi or CLR), assembled contigs, cDNA and long RNA. HISAT2 (install it yourself, 2.2 or newer): short Illumina DNA or RNA-seq reads, including spliced RNA. HISAT2 builds its index once and keeps it in a hidden .repeatviewer_hisat2 folder next to the reference. If the mapper you chose is missing, the run stops with an install hint. Minimap2 settings Setting Default Meaning Map mode ( -x ) map-ont The minimap2 preset. Changing it fills -k and -w Secondaries Primary only Keep only the best place of each read, or also other equally good places ( -N 50 ) Threads CPU count -t Output format PAF PAF (opens as a dotplot) or SAM (opens in the genome browser) Sort and index SAM outputs off Turn the SAM into a coordinate-sorted *.sorted.bam with a .bai index, so the genome browser reads it piece by piece instead of the whole file. Only for SAM output Delete SAM file after sort and index off Keep only the sorted BAM and its index, to save disk space Advanced: -k , -w from the preset Seed length and window Advanced: CIGAR / CS on for PAF -c and --cs=long ; needed to show the differences Advanced: repetitive seeds Default Keep more ( -f 0.00002 ) or Keep all : minimap2 ignores its most frequent seeds by default, which hides hits inside tandem arrays. Use Keep more for satellites Map modes: Long genomic reads : map-ont (ONT), map-pb (PacBio CLR), map-hifi (HiFi), lr:hq (nanopore Q20), map-iclr (Illumina Complete Long Reads). RNA and cDNA : splice (long RNA or cDNA), splice:hq (Iso-Seq), splice:sr (short RNA-seq). Assemblies and short DNA : asm5 , asm10 , asm20 (about 0.1, 1 and 5 % divergence), sr (short genomic reads). HISAT2 settings Setting Default Meaning Library RNA-seq (spliced) Or DNA-seq ( --no-spliced-alignment ) Reads Single-end Or paired-end; R1 and R2 are paired by their file names Strandness Unstranded F or R (single-end), FR or RF (paired-end) Report Best place only Or up to 20 places ( -k 20 ) Sort and index SAM outputs, Delete SAM file after sort and index off As for minimap2 (HISAT2 always writes SAM) Advanced --dta , minimum and maximum intron length, --no-softclip Filters and outputs (Advanced Settings) A placed read is kept only if it passes all filters: minimum identity (default 70 % of aligned columns match), minimum aligned length (25 bp) and minimum mapping quality . Reads that fail, or that do not map, are listed as not used, with the reason. Further settings choose the consensus threshold (majority by default), the minimum variant frequency in the variants table, which files to write, and whether to move the reference annotations onto the mapped reads. Reference names must be unique: mappers report hits by name, so two records with the same name would mix up their hits. RepeatViewer stops with a message if the reference has repeated names."}, {"t": "Mapping: 9.2 What is written", "u": "09-mapping.html#what-is-written", "x": "The mapper's alignment and the contig document sit next to the input. Optional consensus / placed / unused FASTA and the variants table are written beside them when those options are on. A temporary _details/ workspace is used while the run works, then deleted: File Contents <input>_minimap.paf or .sam The mapper's alignment (opens in Preview) <input>_minimap.rvmap Contig-view snapshot of the run …_consensus.fa Consensus of the mapped reads (optional) …_mapped.fa The placed reads, on the reference strand (optional) …_unused.fa Reads not placed (optional) …_variants.tsv Differences table (optional) A <input>_minimap.rvmap (or _hisat ) contig document next to the alignment opens in Contig view. A second run writes _2 ; nothing is overwritten. No samtools or pysam is needed for Mapping itself."}, {"t": "Mapping: 9.3 The genome browser (SAM, BAM, CRAM)", "u": "09-mapping.html#the-genome-browser-sam-bam-cram", "x": "A SAM result opens in the genome browser. BAM and CRAM files from other tools, and GFF, GTF and BED files, open there too. For large mappings, sort and index first (see Samtools ). Figure 9.2. A SAM result in the genome browser: features of the reference on top, coverage, then the reads (blue forward, pink reverse). The navigation bar: back and forward, the file, the sequence (choose another reference sequence), the range shown (type a range to go there), − / + and slider, Select , Fit , Browser ▾ and the side panel button. Reads are drawn as arrows, packed into rows, coloured by strand; differences from the reference are marked on them. Above the reads: the reference ruler, its annotations and the coverage. Hover a read or a position for details; right-click for Copy Position , Zoom to This , Zoom In , Zoom Out , Whole Sequence , and on a read name Copy name , BLAST sequence… . The reference is the project genome when its names match the file; otherwise choose one with Browser ▾ → Choose FASTA… . The choice is remembered for the file. Side panel: Section Settings Display Layout (Packed or one read per row), Sort by, Colour by (Strand, Identity, Mapping quality) Filters Min identity, Mapping quality, Hide secondary placements Annotations Show the reference features, per type Coverage Coverage track options The browser is read-only: it shows, it does not edit."}, {"t": "Mapping: 9.4 Samtools: sort, index, convert", "u": "09-mapping.html#samtools-sort-index-convert", "x": "Tools → Samtools Prepares alignment files (SAM, BAM, CRAM, also .sam.gz ) from RepeatViewer's Mapping or from any other mapper, mostly so that large files open quickly in the genome browser. Tools → Samtools opens the window; use the Sort , Index or Convert tab and Run . Figure 9.3. The Samtools window, Sort tab. Tab Does Output (next to the input) Sort Sorts the alignments by position into a BAM. Index after sort (on by default) also builds the index <name>.sorted.bam and <name>.sorted.bam.bai Index Builds the index of a BAM or CRAM that is already sorted by position .bai (BAM) or .crai (CRAM) Convert Rewrites between SAM, BAM and CRAM without sorting ( Output format , default BAM). Files already in that format are skipped. A CRAM may need its reference <name>.bam , .sam or .cram <name> is the file name without .sam , .bam or .cram . Threads is shared by the three tabs. With an index next to it, a BAM opens in the genome browser window by window, which is much faster for large mappings. Index files follow their BAM or CRAM when you rename it. Samtools work is done with pysam on macOS and Linux ( pip install pysam ), or with a samtools program on the PATH. On Windows, RepeatViewer uses the Win-NGS samtools bundle : download and unzip it, then choose samtools.exe on the Options tab or add its folder to PATH."}, {"t": "Mapping: 9.5 The dotplot (PAF)", "u": "09-mapping.html#the-dotplot-paf", "x": "A PAF result opens as an alignment dotplot: the reads and the reference along the axes, each hit as a line. The Display panel sets which pairs are shown ( Compare ), a name filter, line width and minimum length. This view is useful for seeing where long reads or contigs cross a repeat array. Figure 9.4. A PAF result as an alignment dotplot."}, {"t": "Mapping: 9.6 The report", "u": "09-mapping.html#the-report", "x": "Figure 9.5. The mapping report: placed and reversed reads, mean identity, variant positions, coverage, and the state of each reference feature in the reads. The report shows four numbers at the top (placed, reversed, mean identity, variant positions), the run details, the coverage along the reference with the features under it, and a table of the reference features with how often each is intact , changed (n differences), partial (only part covered) or lost (half or more deleted) in the reads. Then the most frequent differences, with their cytosine context (CG, CHG), where methylation-driven C→T changes are expected. The full list is in …_variants.tsv ."}, {"t": "Trees", "u": "10-trees.html", "x": "Chapter 10 Trees"}, {"t": "Trees: 10.1 Building a tree", "u": "10-trees.html#building-a-tree", "x": "Tools → Tree (add the Tree button in File → Setting → Toolbar ) Builds a phylogenetic tree from the selected FASTA files; several files are combined into one tree. RepeatViewer prepares and checks the input and writes the report; established programs do the computing: MAFFT (bundled) aligns input that is not yet aligned. IQ-TREE 3 (bundled): maximum likelihood, the default. FastME (minimum evolution, with bootstrap) and FastTree (approximate maximum likelihood, for more than about 1,000 sequences): install them yourself (see Installation ). If the chosen program is missing, the run stops with an install hint; it never falls back silently to a weaker method. Figure 10.1. The Tree window, IQ-TREE tab. Tools → Tree opens the window. It has a tab per program ( IQ-TREE , FastME , FastTree ) and Options . Section Setting Default Why Alignment Alignment Align with MAFFT Input whose sequences all have the same length is used as an alignment as it is Monomers: rotate satellite monomers to a common start off Satellite monomers cut from arrays start at different points of the repeat; rotating them first avoids false differences Skip fragments 70 % of the median length Shorter sequences (often 5′-truncated copies) are left out, listed in the report and saved to .excluded.fa . A grey line says how many. Use \"of the longest\" for monomers Settings Branch support SH-aLRT + UFBoot, 1000 each Shown as SH-aLRT/UFBoot; commonly trusted at 80 / 95 and above Model ModelFinder, best by BIC The chosen model is named in the report Methylation check Off Also build a tree without CG (or CG and CHG) positions and compare (see below) Root Unrooted Or an Outgroup… (tick one or more sequences), or midpoint Threads Settings value IQ-TREE picks the best number up to this Fast search ( --fast ) off Quicker IQ-TREE search, for large sets Advanced MAFFT strategy, adjust direction, --op / --ep , independent searches (5), random seed (12345) Several IQ-TREE searches are run and the best kept; the report says how many found the same tree. The seed is recorded so the run can be repeated exactly The ? buttons next to the strategy, method and model explain the choices. Options that do not apply are greyed out. Input checks Copies from the other strand are turned around before aligning ( adjust direction ), so they do not look extremely divergent. Pairs of sequences that share almost no aligned positions stop distance methods; other problems give warnings. If the median difference between sequences is 50 % or more, the report asks whether the input is related at all. Methylation check In plant repeats, cytosines in CG and CHG context are often methylated and mutate to T much faster than other positions. Such changes can group sequences by methylation history instead of descent. With Methylation check on, RepeatViewer also builds a tree without those positions ( _masked.treefile ), compares the two trees (normalised Robinson–Foulds distance) and lists the well-supported groups that change. What is written Like every tool, only the main result goes next to the input: File Contents <input>_iqtree.treefile The tree (Newick with support values), for FigTree, iTOL or ggtree. Opens in the tree viewer <input>_iqtree_details/ Report ( _report.html ), alignment ( .aln.fa ), IQ-TREE report and log, names table ( .names.tsv : tree names and original FASTA headers), methods paragraph with references ( _methods.txt ), program versions and command lines ( _commands.txt ) The engine is part of the name ( _iqtree , _fastme , _fasttree ); rotated or methylation-checked runs add _rot , _cg or _cgchg . A second run writes _2 . If a run fails, nothing half-finished is left and the full log is saved as <input>_iqtree_failed.log , ready to send with a bug report."}, {"t": "Trees: 10.2 The tree viewer", "u": "10-trees.html#the-tree-viewer", "x": "A tree file ( .treefile , .nwk , .newick , .tree ) opens in the tree viewer. After a run the new tree opens there. Figure 10.2. The tree viewer with the Layout, Tip names and Colour tips sections open. The bar above the tree has − / + and slider (zoom), Fit and Export (figure as SVG, PDF or PNG). Shift + scroll zooms; double-click empty space fits. Display panel Section Settings Layout Layout (rectangular, circular, radial…), Order (as in file, or ladderized), Branches (proportional, equal, square root: changes only the drawing), Spread (space between tips), Angle (rotation of circular and radial trees), Flip Tip names Show , Align (all names on one line, with dotted leaders), Text (name, or name plus a trait), Max length (shorten long names from the end or the start), Font Branches Support or length labels, digits, font, line weight, Hide crowded labels, Hide zeros Collapse Collapse clades with low support, or whose tips are closer than a distance (display only) Colour tips By a trait column; Trait table (create, reload, open in a spreadsheet app) Scale bar Show, length, font Statistics Tips, internal nodes, tree depth; Open run report All view settings are saved with the tree (in a hidden .view.json next to it) and used in exported figures. Trait table Tips can be coloured by any property: chromosome, array, subfamily, methylation level, FISH signal. Colour tips → Trait table → Create or update… writes <tree>_traits.tsv next to the tree (first column name ). For trees built by RepeatViewer it is filled from the FASTA headers: chromosome , start , end , strand , array (copies closer than 10 kb), and optionally methylation from a Bismark .cov or modkit bedMethyl file. Add your own columns in a spreadsheet; they are kept when the table is updated. Editing Click a branch to select a clade, or drag over tip names (⌘ adds to the selection). Edit (bottom of the panel), and right-click on the tree, offer: Reroot on this clade , Midpoint root : support values stay on their branches. Swap branches , Collapse clade / Expand clade , Show only this clade , Back to full tree , Expand all . Colour clade… , Remove colour , Remove all colours . Extract sequences of a clade (without gaps, or as aligned). Show in alignment (also a double-click on a tip): opens the alignment the tree was built from at that sequence. Copy name , Copy original header . Save tree (.treefile) , Distance matrix… (patristic distances from the tree, or p-distances from the alignment; copy or save as TSV), Copy Newick . Undo / Redo ( ⌘Z / ⇧⌘Z ). The Find tab searches tip names and trait values and selects the hits. The report Figure 10.3. The tree report: the tree, run details, warnings, methods text and citations. The run report ( _report.html in the details folder; Statistics → Open run report ) shows the tree, the input and alignment numbers, the model, support and rooting, warnings, a methods paragraph you can paste into a paper, and the references to cite."}, {"t": "Repeat analysis", "u": "11-repeat-analysis.html", "x": "Chapter 11 Repeat analysis"}, {"t": "Repeat analysis: 11.1 TE-Aid", "u": "11-repeat-analysis.html#te-aid", "x": "Toolbar: TE-Aid · Tools → Curate → TE-Aid Makes an interactive HTML report for one consensus sequence, in the style of TE-Aid : its copies in the genome and how much of the consensus each covers, the coverage of the consensus by genomic hits, a self-dotplot, and the terminal repeats and ORFs. It is the clearest single view of whether a consensus is finished. Figure 11.1. The TE-Aid window. Section Setting Default Meaning Mode Single genome / Pangenome Single genome Search one genome, or every genome of a pangenome Genome reference genome Or a saved BLAST database Settings Genome BLAST E-value 0.0001 Threshold for finding copies Self BLAST word size 6 Seed length for the self comparison Threads For both BLAST runs Advanced Line transparency Opacity of the hit lines Full-/mid-length hits Colour hits by length relative to the consensus Output: <name>_TEAid.html (one genome) or <name>_pTEAid.html (pangenome), shown in Preview."}, {"t": "Repeat analysis: 11.2 Dotplot", "u": "11-repeat-analysis.html#dotplot", "x": "Toolbar: Dotplot · Tools → Visualize → Dotplot Compares sequences with themselves or with each other. The window has two engines. Figure 11.2. The Dotplot window, Built-in tab. Built-in (fast, interactive): Setting Default Meaning Mode Self comparison Self : each file against itself ( <name>_self_dot.paf ). Pairwise : every sequence against every other ( <first>_multi_dot.paf ); with three or more sequences the self comparisons fill the diagonal Word size 15 Length of exact matches. Larger is faster and less sensitive Include reverse-complement matches on Show inverted repeats (TIRs) as well The result opens in the dotplot view, the same as the Dotplot (Self) tab of a sequence. Flexidot writes publication-style dotplots with Flexidot , and shades the annotations: Setting Meaning Mode One plot for all files, or one per file Output format ( -f ) HTML (interactive) or an image k-mer ( -k ), sequence type ( -t ) Word length; DNA or protein Show GFF features ( -g ) Shade the annotations Advanced: -S , --max_n , -A Mismatches allowed in a word, maximum N content, line width With HTML output, clicking a region in the dotplot jumps the Overview of that sequence to the same position (this needs the RepeatViewer build of Flexidot, installed with RepeatViewer). Outputs: <name>_flexidot.html , <name>_flexidot.gff , <name>_flexidot_legend.png ."}, {"t": "Repeat analysis: 11.3 Repeat Quantify", "u": "11-repeat-analysis.html#repeat-quantify", "x": "Tools → Quantify → Repeat Quantify Measures how much of a genome a library of monomers, satellites or TEs accounts for: copy numbers, arrays, genome fraction and divergence. Aimed at tandem repeats, where \"how many copies, in how many arrays, and how much of the genome\" is the question. Figure 11.3. The Repeat Quantify window. Inputs : the Library (the monomer or consensus FASTA) and the Genome . Add another genome runs the same library against several genomes, one report each. Click files in the Files panel to fill the fields. Engines , one per tab: BLASTn (fast, bundled), RepeatMasker (installed separately; also gives a divergence landscape), or Both , side by side. Setting Default Meaning -word_size (BLASTn) 11 7 can make BLAST hang on large genomes -evalue (BLASTn) 0.0001 -div (RepeatMasker) 40 % Most divergence counted Sensitivity (RepeatMasker) Sensitive ( -s ) RepeatMasker speed preset Advanced: minimum identity, minimum coverage 0 = none -perc_identity and -qcov_hsp_perc Output : a new folder for each run, by default next to the genome: <genome>_repeat_quant_<date>_<time>/ , with an HTML report and TSV and GFF tables. The report has one row per family: Column Meaning monomer_len Length of the library sequence (the period), bp copy_number covered_bp ÷ monomer length (a fraction when less than one monomer is covered) hits Number of alignment pieces arrays Hits merged into continuous blocks (about the number of array loci) covered_bp Genome bp covered, overlaps counted once %target covered_bp as a percentage of the genome mean_array_bp, max_array_bp Mean and longest array mean_identity Length-weighted mean identity to the library sequence (high = young or conserved) mean_divergence RepeatMasker Kimura divergence (high = old) scaffolds Number of genome sequences hit If a family name appears twice in the library, the report warns you: their hits are merged into one row. Give every record a unique name. The genome BLAST index is kept between runs; File → Setting → General → Diagnostics → Clear cache removes it."}, {"t": "Repeat analysis: 11.4 Genome plots: TE density, divergence and abundance", "u": "11-repeat-analysis.html#genome-plots-te-density-divergence-and-abundance", "x": "Toolbar: Visualization · Tools → Visualize Three interactive plots of repeats in a genome: where they sit along the chromosomes ( TE density plot ), how old they are ( TE divergence plot ), and which families dominate ( TE abundance plot ). The density plot needs a reference genome (see Manage Database ). The windows stay open while you click files in the Files panel to fill their fields. The plots open in Preview; Open in browser shows them full size, and the panel on the right of each plot saves it as SVG or PDF. The inputs are a BLAST hit table of your TE library against the genome ( <library>_bn.txt , from BLAST ) or a RepeatMasker .out file. Library names in the form name#class/family (for example Ty3_gypsy_demo#LTR/Gypsy ) let the plots group hits by class. TE density plot (Genome density plot) Tools → Visualize → Genome density plot , or click Visualization on the toolbar. Shows where repeats sit along the chromosomes, as stacked bars per window. Figure 11.4. The TE density plot window, TE tab. The window has a tab per kind of track ( TE , Gene , Methylation ) and Options : Tab Input Accepts TE Reference genome The project genome, or another FASTA DNA from Manage Database TE masking file RepeatMasker .out or a BLAST hit table ( _bn.txt ) TE consensus file The library FASTA (optional: lengths and classes of the families) Gene Annotation file GFF, GTF, BED or VCF (genes, for example) Methylation Methylation file Bismark .cov or modkit bedMethyl Add another … under each field adds more sources; tracks are drawn in field order, the first at the top. Setting Default Meaning Window size 100 kb Width of each bar along the chromosome Y axis content Total TE length (bp) per window What the bar height shows Color by TE order Colour the bars by order, superfamily or family Separate forward / reverse strands off One track per strand Collapse overlapped TEs off Count overlapping hits once Show TE difference track off With two TE sources: a track of where they differ Advanced, Options Which annotation features to draw, minimum methylation coverage, chromosome order, which chromosomes to show, plot all track types together Figure 11.5. A TE density plot of the demo genome: satellite arrays at the chromosome ends, LTR retrotransposons in the middle of each chromosome. In the plot, hover a bar for its numbers, drag to zoom into a region (the region can be saved as its own plot), and use the side panel to switch TE classes on and off, change the scale and save the figure. Output: <name>_bn_den_plot.html (named after the source: bn , RM , …), <name>_multi_dens_plot.html for several sources, and …_region.html for a zoomed region. TE divergence plot Tools → Visualize → TE divergence plot The age profile of the repeats: a histogram of divergence from the consensus (1 − identity), stacked by class. A sharp peak at low divergence means a recent burst; a broad distribution means long, older activity. Figure 11.6. The TE divergence plot window. Setting Meaning TE input files BLAST hit tables or RepeatMasker .out files Bin size Width of each histogram bar (0.005 = 0.5 % divergence) Figure 11.7. A TE divergence plot of the demo genome: young copies near 0, older families further right. Output: <name>_bn_div_plot.html , or <name>_multi_div_plot.html for several sources. TE abundance plot Tools → Visualize → TE abundance plot Which families dominate a genome: families ranked by the genome share they occupy, as a cumulative curve, one curve per RepeatMasker .out file. The table beside the plot lists the families with the bp and percent of the genome they occupy. Figure 11.8. The TE abundance plot window. Setting Meaning RepeatMasker .out files One or more; each becomes a curve Y axis Percentage occupied in genome, or occupied bp Top families How many families to show, ranked by occupied bp Figure 11.9. A TE abundance plot: the cumulative genome share of the demo families, largest first. Output: <name>_RM_abund_plot.html , or <name>_multi_abund_plot.h"}, {"t": "Primers and PCR", "u": "12-primers.html", "x": "Chapter 12 Primers and PCR RepeatViewer designs PCR primers and FISH probes for repeats, shows primers on the sequence, runs in-silico PCR, draws the expected gel, and keeps the lab's primer inventory. Everything runs on your computer; nothing is sent online. Where What Tools → Primers and Probes → Design Design primers ( Primers ), FISH probes for a repeat ( Repetitive Probes ) or painting probes ( Chromosome Painting Probes ) Tools → Primers and Probes → Map Test primers or probes against sequences, like Geneious' Map Primers Tools → Primers and Probes → Database The lab's primer inventory Tools → Primers and Probes → In-silico PCR , Restriction Digest , Gel The products of a PCR, a digest of the sequence, the expected gel Tools → Primers and Probes → Sanger Check , Cloning Check a clone against its Sanger reads; plan putting a product into a vector Every item opens the same tab, Primers and Probes , on the matching section. The toolbar can also show a Primers and Probes button ( File → Setting → Toolbar → Add buttons… ). It offers the same items; right-click designs with the saved settings."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.1 Quick start", "u": "12-primers.html#quick-start", "x": "Click the FASTA you want primers for in the Files panel. Choose Tools → Primers and Probes → Design → Primers . The Primers and Probes tab opens on the Design section, with your sequence. Drag on the map over the part to amplify, keep Mode on Standard PCR and click Design . The candidate pairs appear in the list under the map and as arrows above the sequence. Tick the pairs you want and click Keep Ticked : their primers are now on the sequence. Click the PCR section to see the products of the ticked primers and the expected gel."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.2 The workspace", "u": "12-primers.html#the-workspace", "x": "Primers and Probes is one tab. The row on top holds its sections: Design , Painting , Map , Database , PCR , Digest , Gel , Sanger Check and Cloning . A section keeps what you did in it while you look at another one. Figure 12.1. The Primers and Probes workspace on the Design section: the map, the list under it and the settings in the panel on the right. Design , PCR and Digest work on one sequence: Click a FASTA's name in the Files panel to work on it. Ticking a file's box does not change it. For a file that holds several sequences, Record in the Position row chooses the one to work on. The map shows the sequence. What a section adds is drawn in rows of its own above the bases, so no letter is covered: enzyme names, one row for each candidate pair, and the primer you are editing. A region you chose is a light tint behind them. The list under the map holds what Design found ( candidate ) and the primers already on the sequence ( on the sequence ); in PCR it holds the products. Drag the border between the map and the list to give one of them more room. The panel on the right holds the settings of the section. The other sections are whole pages of their own. Map and Database open without a sequence. Tip. A path field in the panel (for example Avoid file ) is filled by a click in the Files panel: put the cursor in the field first, then click the file. To open a file in a tab of its own while the workspace is in front, right-click it and choose Open in Additional Tab ."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.3 Design", "u": "12-primers.html#design", "x": "The settings are in the panel on the right of the Design section. Run on : Open sequence designs on the open sequence. The candidates appear in the list under the map and nothing is written until you press Keep Ticked or Open Report . Selected files runs the design as a job on every FASTA ticked in the Files panel and writes the report files beside them, with the same settings. Mode is the kind of design. Changing it loads sensible starting values (size, Tm, product size); Restore Defaults brings them back. Mode Use it for Template You get Standard PCR An ordinary pair Any sequence Ranked pairs and a virtual gel qPCR Quantitative PCR The target; optionally a single-copy reference gene FASTA Short amplicons (70 to 180 bp), an optional TaqMan probe, and a matched pair on the reference gene for ΔCt Ladder PCR Showing that a satellite sits in tandem arrays Monomers (one or many) Outward-facing primers inside one monomer. On genomic DNA they give a ladder: 1×, 2×, 3× … the monomer Satellite repeat oligos FISH probes for a satellite or repeat family Monomers or copies, or a TAREAN dimer consensus 35 to 60 nt oligos present in at least Min coverage of the copies, without hairpins, with a Tm for your hybridisation conditions, ready to order with a 5′ label (FAM, Cy3, Cy5 …) Chromosome painting (Chorus2) Oligo pools that paint whole chromosomes or regions The genome FASTA Thousands of single-copy 45 nt oligos along the chromosomes, an order file and a density plot (see below) For ladder PCR and FISH oligos, use many monomers (for example extracted from an array) rather than one: the tool then keeps the parts shared by most copies. A dimer consensus (TAREAN's consensus_dimer.fasta , the monomer written twice) is recognised and cut back to one monomer. Satellite repeat oligos have their own settings: Field Meaning Avoid the other selected files Select the target family first, then the other families (one FASTA each). Probes are designed on the first file and every probe that matches the others is ranked last; the report names the sequences it matches Leave out low-complexity and inverted repeats Low-complexity stretches (DUST, as dustmasker -level 30 ), simple repeats and inverted repeats (arms of 12 nt or more, as EMBOSS einverted ) are found first and no probe may touch them; the report lists them Hybridisation Formamide, SSC and temperature of the hybridisation (default 50 % formamide, 2× SSC, 37 °C) Stringent wash The same for the stringent wash (default 20 % formamide, 0.1× SSC, 42 °C); probes that would melt off in it are not offered Probe Probe concentration in the mix (default 100 nM) For these probes Tm is the Tm under the hybridisation conditions, not in a PCR buffer: SantaLucia (1998) nearest-neighbour values with the Owczarzy et al. (2004) Na⁺ correction (1× SSC = 195 mM Na⁺), minus 0.60 °C per % formamide (Hutton 1977). The default window (48 to 58 °C, about 52 °C for 50 to 60 nt) follows the TAREAN probe design protocol. The report gives each probe's Tm in the wash and how far it stays above the wash temperature. Tip. Never use a PCR product amplified from genomic DNA as a hybridisation probe, even with one band of the expected size: clone and sequence it first, and keep short simple repeats in the clone out of the probe. Primer Conditions : Field Meaning Primer size Minimum, optimum and maximum length (nt) Tm (°C) Minimum, optimum and maximum melting temperature of the binding part GC Allowed GC content (%) Product size Allowed amplicon length (bp) Max Tm difference Largest Tm gap between the two primers of a pair Pairs to return How many pairs the report lists Advanced Settings ▸ adds poly-X, GC clamp, 3′ stability, probe size and Tm, 5′ tails (M13, T7, restriction sites, or your own) and the Tm calculation (salt, Mg²⁺, dNTP and oligo concentration). Options : Ask which pairs to add here (for a run on selected files); Add them to the Database . Restore Defaults brings the starting values of the mode back, Save keeps the s"}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.4 Design on the sequence", "u": "12-primers.html#design-on-the-sequence", "x": "Region : drag on the map or the bases; the range appears in From and to (or type it). Choose what the region is for: - Amplify : the primers sit outside the region, one on each side, and the product covers all of it. - Inside : both primers and the product stay inside the region. - Avoid : no primer may sit in the region (a SNP, a methylated site, a repeat). For probes only Inside and Avoid are offered: a probe sits in the region or keeps out of it. 2. Settings : the mode, primer size, Tm, GC and product size; Advanced has the finer rules and the salt. 3. Design : the pairs appear in the list under the map, best first, and each pair gets a row of arrows above the sequence (the selected pair is filled, the others are outlined). Click a pair to see it; tick the ones you want and press Keep Ticked . They are added to this sequence's primers. Nothing is written before that. Open Report writes the usual primer report. When the region and the settings cannot work together (for example a 1,500 bp region to amplify with products of at most 1,000 bp), an amber note says why before you run, and Fit changes the product range. Nothing is blocked: the settings guide the automatic design, your choice wins."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.5 Primers on the sequence", "u": "12-primers.html#primers-on-the-sequence", "x": "The list under the map shows the primers kept with this FASTA as on the sequence . Each is drawn as an arrow where it binds (blue: points right; orange: points left). Add Primer types a new one, From Database picks saved ones. From Selection makes a primer from bases on the sequence: drag over 10 to 60 bases on the map, then choose Forward Primer (the bases as they are) or Reverse Primer (their reverse complement). The primer appears on the sequence and can be dragged or edited like any other. When the selection does not fit, the note by the list says why. Click a primer to see its details in the panel on the right and jump to it: Tm, hairpin and self-dimer ΔG, where and how well it binds. While a primer is selected the design settings are folded to one line, Design Settings ; Show opens them again. Clicking a candidate also brings them back. The details sum up the dimers with the other ticked primers in one line (for example Dimers: 6 checked, all ok ) and list only the pairs to look at. Show All lists every pair, Hide folds the good ones away again. Hover the list to read the full primer names. The selected primer is drawn as a bar in its own row right above its bases. Drag the bar to move the primer, or drag one of its ends to make it longer or shorter. A base that does not match the template is red on the primer. Snap to Tm shortens or lengthens the primer along the template to reach a Tm; Fix Mismatches to Match Template makes it match this sequence; Edit Primer opens the primer with its tail and checks; Sites in Genome shows where it binds in a genome. Right-click a primer in the list to edit it, copy its sequence, add it to the Primer Database or remove it. The tick marks the primers that the PCR section uses. A tail is the 5′ part that does not bind (lower-case letters). It is copied into the product from cycle 3 on, so two Tm values are shown: annealed (the binding part, mismatches included) and full (the whole oligo). Edit Primer shows the primer on its best site, with mismatches in red and matching 3′ bases in green; Site steps through other sites; − + make either end shorter or longer along the template; 5′ tail adds a restriction site (with padding bases so the enzyme cuts), a sequencing or promoter tail, or your own; and the dimer and hairpin checks give ΔG at 37 °C (below −9 kcal/mol for dimers or −3 for hairpins is a warning). Sites in Genome : each genome sequence drawn on one scale, forward sites as ticks above the bar, reverse sites below (darker = fewer mismatches), and for a qPCR pair the number of products per haploid genome, the value to expect in a copy-number assay. Figure 12.2. A primer selected on the sequence: its bar above the bases and its details in the panel. Primers are saved in a hidden name.primers_RV.json next to the FASTA and follow it when it is renamed or moved."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.6 In-silico PCR", "u": "12-primers.html#in-silico-pcr", "x": "Tools → Primers and Probes → In-silico PCR , or the PCR section of the workspace. The products are listed under the map and drawn in the expected gel in the panel. There is no Run button: the products update by themselves when you change anything. Figure 12.3. The PCR section: the products under the map, and the primers, the expected gel and the options in the panel. The panel, from top to bottom: Section What it holds Run on Input : Open sequence (the sequence in the map, live) or Selected files (every FASTA ticked in the Files panel, so one pair can be tested on many satellite families at once; the products of all files are listed together, each with its file) Primers Forward and Reverse : primers on this sequence. From Database puts saved primers on the sequence so they can be chosen. Kind , Mismatches , 3′ end exact and Polymerase set the reaction Products Every product the two primers make, best first: Range , Size and mm F / R (mismatches of the forward and the reverse primer at that site). Click a product to see it on the sequence Expected gel The products next to a size ladder Options Open in a Gel , Bench Card , Save Products Kind : Standard lists every product. Satellite ladder groups the products that step by a monomer and labels them 1×, 2×, 3×. Mismatches (none, or up to 1 to 4 per primer) and 3′ end exact (no rule, or the last 2 to 5 bases) decide which sites count. Polymerase sets how fast long products fall off. The status box under the primers says what to expect. Green: Ready for PCR , with the expected product size. Amber: the expected product is longer or shorter than the product range set in Design. That is allowed (the tube still makes it); Set Range to … bp changes the range in Design to fit. No product means the two primers do not face each other on this sequence. Open in a Gel makes a gel document from this PCR, next to the sequence, that you can change (lanes, agarose, ladder; see The gel ). Save Products writes, next to the sequence, <template>_PCR_<primers>.fa (the products) and …_details/ ( _products.tsv , _history.json , _result.json ). The products file opens like any multi-FASTA, with the primer sites and the template's features on each product. Figure 12.4. The products of a satellite ladder PCR: 1×, 2×, 3× … monomers, with the primers and the monomer annotations carried over. Bench Card : volumes per reaction and for all reactions plus extra (template added to each tube); annealing from the lower primer Tm (Taq 3 °C below, Phusion or Q5 3 °C above); extension from the longest product wanted (Taq 1 min per kb, Phusion or Q5 20 s per kb); freezer places from the Primer Database. Change the numbers on the right, then Save PDF or Print ."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.7 Restriction digest", "u": "12-primers.html#restriction-digest", "x": "Tools → Primers and Probes → Restriction Digest , or the Digest section of the workspace. The enzymes that cut the sequence are named in a row above the bases on the map and listed in the panel. Figure 12.5. The Digest section: enzyme sites on the map and the list of enzymes in the panel. Section What it holds Enzyme set Set : which enzymes to look at. Methylation-sensitive holds the enzymes with known CG or CHG sensitivity (HpaII and MspI as a pair). Show : enzymes that cut this often are ticked and drawn on the sequence Methylation Data : a Bismark .cov or modkit bedMethyl file. Methylated ≥ and Reads ≥ : a site counts as blocked when a cytosine of the site is methylated at least this much, with at least this many reads, in a context that blocks the enzyme Enzymes The list: Enzyme , Site , Cuts (sites on this sequence) and In your data (sites probably not cut because of methylation in your file) Click an enzyme to go to its first site. Tick or untick any enzyme; only the ticked ones are drawn and digested. Select All ticks every enzyme in the list; press it again to untick them all. With methylation data, the rows of the chromosome named like this sequence are read (an extracted region named chrom:start-end is shifted to its own positions). Blocked sites are drawn red and dashed: probably not cut . Without data the map shows the labels only. The sensitivity of each enzyme (CG, CHG, Dam, Dcm) follows the Promega and NEB tables and REBASE (McClelland et al. 1994). Dam and Dcm only matter for DNA grown in E. coli . Digest shows the ticked enzymes as a gel, and the panel changes to the result. Enzymes on top goes back to the list. Figure 12.6. The result of a digest: one lane for each ticked enzyme, and the bands of the chosen lane. Section What it holds Digest Lanes : one for each ticked enzyme; with methylation data, an enzyme with blocked sites gets a second lane without the data, to compare. Array : the repeat period, from monomer annotations (a name with three or more copies), otherwise estimated from the sequence. Site loss and Completeness (below) Gel Agarose and Ladder ( Automatic or one you choose). Brightness follows DNA mass; very many faint bands of nearby sizes run as a smear Lane The brightest bands of the chosen lane: Size ( n× is n monomers) and Brightness (the share of the lane's DNA in that band) Site loss : From the copies uses the sites as they are in this sequence, so copies that lost the site by mutation give the 2×, 3× … ladder. A site in every copy adds the missing sites at the array period. Completeness : the share of molecules cut at an open site. With methylation data, each site is cut with completeness × (1 − share methylated in a blocking context). Open in a Gel writes a gel document you can change; Compare with Gel Photo puts your real gel next to this one (see The gel )."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.8 The gel", "u": "12-primers.html#the-gel", "x": "A .rvgel file opens as an agarose gel, in the Gel section of the workspace (every Open in a Gel button, Tools → Primers and Probes → Gel , or a click on a gel in the Files panel while the workspace is in front). Every lane is a ladder, the products of a PCR run, or products cut with a restriction enzyme. A gel that is a file is saved as it changes. A new, empty gel ( Tools → Primers and Probes → Gel ) holds only a ladder and is not a file yet. Save → Gel writes it as a .rvgel in the current folder; from then on it saves itself like any gel. If you leave a new gel that you changed and did not save, RepeatViewer asks first: Save , Discard (throw it away) or Cancel (stay on the gel). Figure 12.7. The in-silico gel of a satellite ladder PCR: GeneRuler 1 kb Plus next to the 1×, 2×, 3× … products. Control What it does Add Lane , Duplicate , Remove , ◀ ▶ Edit and order the lanes Ladder The size marker (GeneRuler, NEB and others) Agarose Which sizes separate: 0.7 % for 1 to 12 kb, 3 % for 50 to 1,000 bp Run How far the front got; small bands can run off a long run Look Dark (SYBR, GelRed) or light (stained) gel Brightness by amount of DNA A band's brightness follows its mass (amount × length), as with SYBR, so short products look fainter Show primer-dimer Draw the primer-dimer smear Cut with (per lane) Digest the lane's products with a restriction enzyme Save Gel (the .rvgel document), or the figure as SVG, PNG (300 dpi) or PDF Hover a band to see what it is, click to select it, double-click to open the products file. Compare with Gel Photo puts a picture of your real gel (PNG, JPG or TIFF) next to the in-silico one. Lanes and bands are found for you. Choose which photo lane holds the ladder (its bands give the size scale) and which gel lane each photo lane should match. Every band is then named: matched (within 7 %), off-target (a predicted off-target product), unexplained (try the PCR with more mismatches), and expected bands not seen ."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.9 Map: test primers on sequences", "u": "12-primers.html#map-test-primers-on-sequences", "x": "Tools → Primers and Probes → Map → Primers (or Repetitive Probes ). It opens as the Map section of the workspace, with its settings on the right. Figure 12.8. The Map section. Inputs as in Design. Test primers on : every sequence, or the consensus of an alignment. Primers : Test specific primers (type a sequence or pick a saved one), or Search for saved primers that match (from the Primer Database or a primers FASTA; forward, reverse, probe; pairs only). Products : optional included and target regions, product size range, optimal size. Mismatches : maximum mismatches, and how many 3′ bases must match exactly. Advanced Settings : Tm conditions, and counting sites and products in a genome (both strands; every site within the allowed mismatches is found, also on a whole genome). Output: <name>_primer_map.html with binding sites, predicted products and a gel."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.10 Database: the lab's primer inventory", "u": "12-primers.html#database-the-labs-primer-inventory", "x": "The Database is the record of every primer the lab has: sequence and Tm, what it amplifies and in which organism, where the tube is, whether it is in stock and whether it worked, who ordered it and when, and every change since. Figure 12.9. The Database section. It opens as the Database section of the workspace. The page Filter searches every field; the boxes next to it show one organism, one person or one status. Select a primer to edit it below the table, then Save Changes . Select several to set a field on all of them at once (for example a new box, or Used up ). History lists every change to the selected primer: who, when, what. Pair is found from the names ( _for / _rev , _fwd / _rev1 , _FW / _RV , _F / _R ); write it in Pairs with when the names do not show it. Two Tm columns : Tm Primer3 is calculated by RepeatViewer; Tm supplier is the value from the synthesis report. They differ because each uses its own method and concentrations. Lab holds the lists the fields choose from: People (with roles; rename, merge two entries for one person, or remove; people who ordered primers stay as former members), Places (rooms, freezers, boxes), Organisms and Targets . Find Duplicates : the same sequence under two names, reverse complements, one primer inside another. Export : the whole table (TSV), a FASTA, or an order sheet. Import : a CSV, TSV or Excel sheet. The header row is found and known columns are read (name, sequence, organism, target, Tm, dissolved volume, ordered by, ordered on, box, freezer, room, status, tested, annealing, concentration, pair, note). Before importing it shows what is new, what is already there (same sequence under another name is kept as Other names ) and which rows are skipped. Importing again later only adds what is new. Sharing with the lab The button at the bottom left says where the database is: Database: This Computer ▾ , or the name of a shared folder. One person: Database ▾ → Shared Database… and choose the lab's folder in a synced cloud folder (Sciebo, Dropbox). If there is no database there yet, RepeatViewer offers to make one and to copy your primers into it. Everyone else: Database ▾ → Shared Database… and choose the same lab folder in their own synced folder. If the shared database cannot be found (not synced yet, moved), a bar says so and offers Try Again or Use This Computer ; nothing switches silently. The first time you change something, you are asked for your name; it appears in the history ( Lab → Change Your Name… ). Each person and computer writes only its own change log, so the cloud service never has to choose between two versions of a file and no change is lost. If two people change the same field of one primer, the later change wins. Check a new sequence against the stock Before designing and ordering for a new plant, see what the lab already has: select the primers to test (none = all) and click Check a Sequence . Give the Sequence (click a FASTA in the Files panel) and how strict to be (product size; mismatches, by default up to 2 but with the last 3 bases at the 3′ end matching). The report starts with From your Primer Database : Ready pairs : two saved primers that give a product, with size, mismatches and where the tubes are. Saved pairs, exact matches and pairs that worked before come first. Single primers that bind : no saved partner gives a product; one new partner is enough."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.11 Chromosome painting (Chorus2)", "u": "12-primers.html#chromosome-painting-chorus2", "x": "Choose Tools → Primers and Probes → Design → Chromosome Painting Probes . It opens as the Painting section of the workspace. Chorus2 (Zhang et al. 2021) finds oligos that occur once in the genome along every chromosome. RepeatViewer runs it, or reads what it produced on a server, and then picks the pool you order. Chorus2 tab : Run Chorus2 here : give the Genome ; optionally paint only one chromosome or region. A genome of a few Gb takes hours and much memory: a server is better. Import results from the server : choose the Chorus2 BED. Chorus2 settings: probe length (45 nt), maximum homology (75 %), minimum dTm (10 °C), k-mer step (5 bp), ploidy, threads. NGS Filter (optional): genomic reads give each probe a k-mer score; probes in the lowest and highest score quantiles (default 10 % and 90 %) are dropped. Copy Server Command : the exact command lines for the server. Install Chorus2 once, in its own conda environment (it needs an older pandas): conda create -n chorus2 -c conda-forge -c bioconda python=3.9 \"pandas<2\" numpy cython pyfasta \"primer3-py<2\" pybigwig pybedtools kmer-jellyfish bwa conda run -n chorus2 pip install --no-deps git+https://github.com/zhangtaolab/Chorus2.git On a server: conda activate chorus2 Chorus2 -g genome.fa -i genome.fa -s chorus2_out -t 16 -l 45 --homology 75 -d 10 --step 5 --ploidy 2 --docker True Copy chorus2_out/genome.fa.bed back (or probes_kmer_score.bed after the NGS filter) and import it. On Windows, run Chorus2 on a Linux server and import. Selection tab : Field Meaning Min gap between probes Neighbouring probes at least this far apart (25 bp) Strand Both (every second probe reverse-complemented, the Chorus2 default) or Plus Amount All probes , a Density (probes per kb; painting usually needs 0.1 to 1 per kb), or a Pool size (total number, spread evenly) Regions Keep probes only here, for example chr1, chr2:1-5000000 Pool name, 5′ / 3′ adapter Used in oligo names; adapters (for example T7) go into the order file only Results : genome.fa_painting.html report: counts, density plot, per chromosome, methods genome.fa_painting_details/ genome.fa_painting_probes.bed probes (BED, for example for IGV) genome.fa_painting_probes.fa probe sequences genome.fa_painting_order.tsv order file (adapter + probe + adapter) genome.fa_painting.json all results chorus2/ Chorus2's own files (when run here) Painting pools are not added to the Primer Database (they hold thousands of oligos); use the order file. Too few probes for the pool size: lower the gap, widen the regions or the NGS score window, or rerun Chorus2 with a smaller k-mer step or a higher maximum homology."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.12 Sanger Check", "u": "12-primers.html#sanger-check", "x": "Tools → Primers and Probes → Sanger Check checks a clone against its Sanger reads: each read is trimmed by quality, aligned to the sequence the clone should carry, and every difference is shown with its chromatogram. Figure 12.10. The Sanger Check section: the reads, what they cover of the expected sequence, and the chromatogram around a difference. Section What it holds Expected Sequence : the sequence the clone should carry (click a FASTA in the Files panel, or Browse). Record chooses one when the file holds several Reads Files : the .ab1 files from sequencing (click them in the Files panel, or Browse). Plain .seq or FASTA reads work too, but without a chromatogram or quality. Clear takes all reads off the list. Save Reads as FASTA writes the good part of every read as a FASTA file beside it Quality Trim cutoff : how strictly the ends are trimmed (Mott's rule, as phred uses). Real from Q : a difference below this quality is doubtful (the machine, not the clone) Options Write a report beside the reads (an HTML page); Restore Defaults , Save (keep these settings) and Check The expected sequence is optional. Without one, click an .ab1 read to look at its chromatogram and quality, or save the reads as FASTA. With an expected sequence, each read is aligned in both directions: The table lists every read: Read , Direction , Covers , Identity , Difference and Quality . Coverage of the expected sequence shows which part each read covers and where the differences are. Amber marks what no read reached: the clone is only confirmed where a read went. Click a difference to see the chromatogram around it. The four dyes are G black, A green, T red, C blue; the called base is on top, the expected one underneath when they differ. A clean single peak with a high quality score is a real difference in the clone; a double peak, a shoulder or a low score is a poor call. The verdict under the boxes is green only when the reads cover every base and nothing is left over."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.13 Cloning", "u": "12-primers.html#cloning", "x": "Tools → Primers and Probes → Cloning plans putting a PCR product into a vector: the plasmid size in both orientations and the band a positive colony shows in colony PCR. Figure 12.11. The Cloning section: both orientations of the insert and the colony PCR gel. Section What it holds Insert Sequence : the PCR product, with the primer tails already on it (click a FASTA in the Files panel, or Browse). Record chooses one when the file holds several Ligation Vector : pGEM-T Easy, pJET1.2/blunt or your own. Method : TA needs the 3′ A that Taq leaves; Blunt needs a proofreading product; Restriction uses the sites in the primer tails, and shows Enzymes for the two ends. Polymerase Your vector Length of the vector, and Empty PCR : the product your colony-PCR pair gives on the vector with no insert (the vector's map or manual says it) Options Gel saves the colony PCR gel; Report saves a report of this cloning plan The table lists both orientations of the insert ( Orientation , Insert , Plasmid , Colony PCR ). A TA or blunt ligation gives both; two different enzymes give only the first. The colony PCR product is the empty-vector product plus the insert, so a colony with the insert runs that much higher than one without. With the empty-vector product given, the page also tells how far a sequencing read gets into the insert. Tip. No vector sequence ships with RepeatViewer, so every size here comes from the numbers you give, never from a guessed map."}, {"t": "Primers and PCR: 12.14 How it works", "u": "12-primers.html#how-it-works", "x": "Primer picking, Tm, and the hairpin and dimer checks use Primer3 (primer3-py). The binding-site search (Map, coverage on copies, genome counts) is RepeatViewer's own, on both strands: small inputs are scored at every position; on a genome, every version of each primer's 3′ end within the allowed mismatches is looked up, so the same sites are found. Ladder PCR and FISH oligos build on these. Ladder primers are picked on the dimer with Primer3's template mispriming check switched off, because every primer in a tandem repeat binds the template many times. Chromosome painting uses Chorus2 (MIT licence) in its own conda environment. References: Untergasser et al. (2012) Nucleic Acids Research 40:e115 (Primer3). Owczarzy R et al. (2004) Biochemistry 43:3537–3554 (salt correction). Hutton JR (1977) Nucleic Acids Research 4:3537–3555 (formamide). Morgulis A et al. (2006) Journal of Computational Biology 13:1028–1040 (DUST). Zhang T, Liu G, Zhao H, Braz GT, Jiang J (2021) Plant Biotechnology Journal 19:1967–1978 (Chorus2)."}, {"t": "Automation", "u": "13-automation.html", "x": "Chapter 13 Automation"}, {"t": "Automation: 13.1 Workflows", "u": "13-automation.html#workflows", "x": "Workflows → Design and manage your workflows A workflow chains tools so that a multi-step pass runs as one action. If you always follow BLAST with MSA Extend and Align, make it a workflow and run it on many files at once. Each step uses that tool's saved settings, so set the parameters in the tool's own window first. Figure 13.1. The workflow designer with the built-in Blast-Extract-Extend-Align workflow. The list on the left holds your workflows. New , Rename , Duplicate and Delete manage them. Blast-Extract-Extend-Align (BEEA) is built in. The right side lists the steps in the order they run. Add steps from the Sequences , Tools and Plugins menus; Remove deletes the selected step. Tick a step to keep its output files after the workflow finishes; unticked intermediate files are removed. The last step's result is always kept. Save stores the workflow. It then appears in the Workflows menu: select files and choose it to run. Steps only connect when the output of one fits the input of the next (for example, BLAST makes FASTA that Align takes). Tools that make reports or plots (TE-Aid, Dotplot) end a workflow. Steps that need the genome do not run until a reference genome is set."}, {"t": "Automation: 13.2 Plugins", "u": "13-automation.html#plugins", "x": "Tools → Plugin (Beta) → Manage plugin Plugins add tools written in Python that use the same file selection, progress bar and output highlighting as the built-in ones. A plugin receives the selected files and the project (genome, databases, thread count) and returns the files it made. Add installs a plugin package: a folder with a pyproject.toml , or a .whl file. Installed plugins appear under Tools → Plugin (Beta) and can be put on the toolbar. See PLUGINS.md for the programming interface and PLUGINS_TE_BENCHMARK.md for a worked example; example plugins are in the repository's examples/ folder."}, {"t": "Automation: 13.3 Command wrappers", "u": "13-automation.html#command-wrappers", "x": "Tools → Command wrapper → Manage wrapper A command wrapper turns any command line you use often into a RepeatViewer tool, without writing code. Click New , give the wrapper a unique name, an optional tooltip, and the command, using these placeholders: Placeholder Filled with ${RV_INPUT} Full path of the first selected file ${RV_INPUTS} Full paths of all selected files, separated by spaces ${RV_GENOME} Full path of the reference genome (empty if none) ${RV_FOLDER} The folder shown in the Files panel ${RV_OUTDIR} Where to write: the folder of the selected file ${RV_STEM} File name without its last extension ( TE123.fasta → TE123 ) ${RV_NAME} File name with its extension Example: blastn -query ${RV_INPUT} -db ${RV_GENOME} -out ${RV_OUTDIR}/${RV_STEM}_b.txt -evalue 1e-5 -outfmt 6 You can also define your own variables (shown as fields when the wrapper runs) and declare which output files the command makes, so they are highlighted and opened like the outputs of built-in tools. Wrappers appear under Tools → Command wrapper and can go on the toolbar. Figure 13.2. The command wrapper manager."}, {"t": "Automation: 13.4 Command line", "u": "13-automation.html#command-line", "x": "repeatviewer [-i FOLDER] [-g GENOME.fa] Option Meaning -i , --input-dir Folder to open at start -g , --genome Reference genome FASTA --version Print the version and quit From a source folder, python RepeatViewer.py works too. Folders, layout, genome and settings are remembered between sessions, so the options are for scripted or repeated launches."}, {"t": "Settings", "u": "14-settings.html", "x": "Chapter 14 Settings File → Setting ( ⌘, on macOS) has two tabs: General and Toolbar . Save applies the changes; Restore defaults resets the tab. The path of the settings folder is shown at the bottom of the window. Figure 14.1. Settings, General tab."}, {"t": "Settings: 14.1 General", "u": "14-settings.html#general", "x": "Section Setting What it does Appearance Theme Auto (follows the system), Light or Dark Accent colour The colour of selections and highlights Files columns Show columns Off: the Files panel shows only names. On: also the columns ticked below Columns Length, number of sequences, GC %, type, description, modified, size, colour, cytogenetic images Sequence tools Use system sequence viewer Open alignments in an external viewer such as AliView, and where it is Files & preview Folders / Files font Font size in the Folders and Files panels Preview font: text Font size of the Text view Preview zoom: HTML, PDF, image Default zoom for reports, PDFs and images Default preview fit Fit window, fit width, or original size Default preview tab Which view a FASTA opens in: Text, Overview or Dotplot (Self) Show length and GC% in the preview Off makes large genome FASTA files open faster, because GC % needs every base Performance Threads for the UI Threads kept free so the window stays responsive; tools use the rest Max. parallel jobs How many tool runs (or selected files) run at the same time Startup Reopen last folder Open the last folder and genome at start Restore window size Restore the window size and position Splash screen Show the start-up banner with tips External tools Conda env name or path The conda environment (on Windows: the WSL2 environment) with RepeatMasker, FastGA, HISAT2 and other external programs. Test shows which are found BLAST databases Base folder Where new BLAST indexes are written. Manage Database opens the database window Cytogenetic images Experimenter Your name, stored with images you attach Diagnostics Verbose logging Write detailed messages to the log file Open log file, Open config folder Show the log and settings files Copy diagnostics Copy the version, system and folder locations to the clipboard, for a bug report Clear cache Delete cached data (such as BLAST indexes kept by Repeat Quantify); shows how much space it uses"}, {"t": "Settings: 14.2 Toolbar", "u": "14-settings.html#toolbar", "x": "Figure 14.2. Settings, Toolbar tab. Drag the entries to change the button order. Toolbar font size sets the size of the button labels. Add buttons… adds any tool from the File, Sequence and Tools menus (for example Mapping, Tree, Primers and Probes, NCBI Download, HMMER, AliView) or a separator; Remove takes the selected button off. Reset to default restores BLAST, Align, Dotplot, TE-Aid, Mapping, Tree, Primers and Probes and Visualization. When the window is too narrow for all buttons, the rest move into a menu behind the arrow at the right end of the toolbar."}, {"t": "Reference", "u": "15-reference.html", "x": "Chapter 15 Reference"}, {"t": "Reference: 15.1 Output files", "u": "15-reference.html#output-files", "x": "All outputs are written next to their input unless noted. <name> is the full input file name with its extension, for example reads.fa → reads.fa_mafft.fa . A repeated run adds _2 , _3 . Pattern Made by Contents <name>.seqmap_RV.json (hidden) Every annotation The annotations of <name> <name>.primers_RV.json (hidden) Primers tab Primers kept with the sequence <name>_RV.gff3 , .gtf , .bed Export Annotations <name>_annotation.pdf , .svg , .png Export Figure of the current view <name>_bn.txt ( _bp , _bx ) BLAST Hit table <name>_bn.txt.fa BLAST Hit regions cut out of the genome <name>_bn.gff BLAST Hit positions (optional) <name>_obn.html , _obn.txt Online BLAST NCBI report and hit table <name>_hmmer.txt HMMER Hit table <name>_mafft.fa , <name>_muscle.fa Align, one file Alignment <name>_cmafft.fa , <name>_cmuscle.fa Align, combined Alignment of all selected files <name>_FastGA.paf , <name>_FastGA_vs_<ref>.paf , <first>_multi_FastGA.paf FastGA Alignments for the dotplot <name>_gap.fa MSA Clean Gappy columns removed <name>_rowdiv.fa MSA Clean Divergent rows removed <name>_rowgap.fa MSA Clean Gappy rows removed <name>_<threshold>_co.fa Consensus Consensus sequence <name>_extr.fa Extract Sequences Extracted records <name>_rc.fa Reverse Complement Reverse complement <name>_orfs.fa ORF Prediction ORFs <name>_translated.faa Translate Protein translation <accession>.fa , .gb , .faa , .gp Download from NCBI Downloaded records <name>_TEAid.html , <name>_pTEAid.html TE-Aid Report (one genome, pangenome) <name>_self_dot.paf , <first>_multi_dot.paf Dotplot, built-in Dotplot data <name>_flexidot.html , .gff , _legend.png Dotplot, Flexidot Dotplot and its legend <name>_msa_plot.png , .pdf MSA plot Alignment figure <name>_minimap.paf or .sam , <name>_minimap.rvmap Mapping Alignment and contig view; optional …_consensus.fa / …_mapped.fa beside them <name>.sorted.bam , .bai , .crai Samtools Sort and Index, Mapping Sorted alignment and its index <name>.bam , .sam , .cram Samtools Convert Alignment in another format <name>_iqtree.treefile , <name>_iqtree_details/ Tree Tree; report, alignment, logs <tree>_traits.tsv Tree viewer Trait table <tree>_distances_patristic.tsv , _pdistance.tsv Tree viewer Distance matrices <genome>_repeat_quant_<date>/ Repeat Quantify Report and tables <name>_bn_den_plot.html , _multi_dens_plot.html TE density plot Plot <name>_bn_div_plot.html , _multi_div_plot.html TE divergence plot Plot <name>_RM_abund_plot.html , _multi_abund_plot.html TE abundance plot Plot <name>_primers.html , <name>_primers_details/ Primer Design Report, primers, pairs, gel <name>_primer_map.html Map Primers Binding sites and products <name>_PCR_<primers>.fa , …_details/ In-silico PCR Products; tables and history <name>.rvgel In-silico PCR, gel Gel document <genome>_painting.html , _painting_details/ Chromosome painting Report, probes, order file <name>_<tool>_failed.log Tree, Mapping Full log of a failed run"}, {"t": "Reference: 15.2 Troubleshooting", "u": "15-reference.html#troubleshooting", "x": "A tool says the genome is not set. Choose File → Manage Database… , add the genome FASTA as FASTA DNA and click Set as reference genome . BLAST, MSA Extend, TE-Aid, boundary prediction (Genome Cov) and the genome plots need it. RepeatMasker, FastGA, HISAT2, FastME or FastTree is not found. They are not bundled. Install them into a conda environment (see Installation ), enter that environment in File → Setting → General → External tools , and click Test . On Windows they must be in a WSL2 environment. BLAST or Repeat Quantify is very slow. Check the word size: small values are much more sensitive and much slower on repetitive genomes. For Repeat Quantify, 11 is a good BLASTn default and 7 can make it hang. Also check Threads for the UI in File → Setting → General → Performance : a high value leaves few threads for the tools. Minimap2 misses hits inside a satellite array. minimap2 ignores its most frequent seeds. In Mapping → Advanced Settings , set repetitive seeds to Keep more . A mapping gives differences at 100 % identity. The reference has two records with the same name. Give every record a unique name. A job cannot be stopped at once. Stop in the status bar cancels the running job; external programs may take a moment to end. An HTML report is blank in Preview. Use Open in browser in the Preview title bar. New outputs are not visible. Press F5 to refresh, and check that you are looking at the folder of the input: outputs go there. A Repeat Quantify report says a name appears twice in the library. Two records share a name; their hits were merged into one row. Rename them. macOS refuses to open the app. For an unsigned build, right-click the app, choose Open , and confirm. This is needed only the first time. Reporting a bug. File → Setting → General → Diagnostics → Copy diagnostics copies the version, system and folder locations. Switch on Verbose logging , repeat the problem, and attach the file from Open log file . For failed Tree and Mapping runs, attach the _failed.log next to the input."}, {"t": "Reference: 15.3 Citation", "u": "15-reference.html#citation", "x": "If RepeatViewer contributes to your work, please mention RepeatViewer for curation, analysis and visualisation, and cite TEtrimmer ( Nature Communications , 2025) where its methods are used. Tool reports (Tree, Mapping, Primers) include a methods paragraph and the references of the programs they ran; cite those programs too."}, {"t": "Reference: 15.4 Bundled programs", "u": "15-reference.html#bundled-programs", "x": "BLAST+, MAFFT, MUSCLE, minimap2 and IQ-TREE are distributed with RepeatViewer under their own licences. The NOTICE.md file next to each program in repeatviewer/backends/ gives its licence and source."}];